hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"7866","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Nothing breaks his jiwar except intercourse itself, like fasting and hajj."
"7867","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Shabi, about a woman who vowed to observe itikaf fifty days, then her husband sent her back, he said: She makes up what remains upon her."
"7868","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: An opponent comes to him in his mujawara. He said: Let him defend himself and dispute with him."
"7869","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if this mujawir is brought goods in his tent to sell or buy, may he do it? He said: Yes, he sells in his mujawara."
"7870","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if he comes to the market to look at all. He said: I dislike that, it is only remembrance and worship. I said: He writes in his mujawara to an amir seeking worldly gain, or to his slave? He said: There is no harm in that."
"7871","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: There is no harm if the mutakif disputes with the amir in the mosque, or settles with a debtor in the mosque."
"7872","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Was it said: He does not enter a house, nor pass under a roof under a threshold? He said: Yes. Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar said it."
"7873","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The mujawir at the door of the mosque, may he sit under a shade? He said: Yes. I said: So he is under a roof? He said: The mosque is not like anything. A man said: If he goes to relieve himself? He said: In the mountains and on high grounds. I said: A mujawir inside the mosque, may he make his tent at its door for his need if he wishes? He said: Yes. I said: What if he goes to relieve himself, may he pass under a roof? He said: No. I said: May he pass under a vaulted arch, or stones with no threshold in it, nor wood? He said: Yes. I said to Abu Bakr: What is qabw? He said: An arch."
"7874","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar told me about the vaulted arch, he said: What threshold is worse than the vaulted arch? I said: A hewn stone? He said: Yes, that is a threshold, let him not pass under it. I said to Ata: Shall I pitch a tent at the door of the mosque, staying in jiwar in it? He said: Yes. I said: It is a threshold. He said: No harm. I said: Shall I make it of sticks, then put its covering over it? He said: Yes, let him pass under it if he wishes. He said: And that is not in a building."
"7875","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata explained to me the difference between the jiwar of the villager and the Bedouin. He said: As for the villager, when he vows jiwar, he leaves his house, and leaves his wife, and fasts. And the Bedouin is not of the people of Mecca, so when he vows jiwar, all Mecca is then his place, so he stays in jiwar in whichever parts of Mecca he wishes, and in whichever of its houses he wishes, and he does not fast, and he may have women if he wishes, and sell and buy, and attend gatherings, and enter houses, and visit the sick, and follow the funeral, unless he intends in himself that his jiwar be at the door of the mosque, and keeps away from what is forbidden in mujawara. And he counted the people of Arafa among the people of Mecca, and recited: That is for one whose family is not present at the Sacred Mosque. He said: And we heard that said. I said: May he go out to his family for a need in a settled matter? He said: No. I said: Why does he perform hajj? Why does he perform umra? Why do they differ? He said: Hajj and umra are better than what he is in."
"7876","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, that he said about the Bedouin: When he vows jiwar, and does not intend it at the door of the mosque, then he stays in jiwar in whichever of the town he wishes."
"7877","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, he said: One who is not of its people stays in jiwar wherever he wishes of it, and its people stay at the door of the mosque if he intended itikaf at its door. And sleeping in the mosque is disliked."
"7878","From Fudayl, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: The whole Haram is a mosque, he observes itikaf in whichever of it he wishes, and if he wishes in his house, except that he does not pray except in congregation."
"7879","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata about a man who vowed jiwar for a year. He said: Let him perform hajj, and make up what he was absent in hajj, and not begin a future year."
"7880","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: When a woman menstruates while in itikaf, she goes out to her house, and when she is pure she makes up that."
"7881","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When she menstruates while in itikaf, she returns to her house, and when she is pure let her return to her jiwar. Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar said it."
"7882","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Her husband should not touch her until she finishes her jiwar."
"7883","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: She became pure in part of the day? He said: Let her go that day, and not count that day."
"7884","From Fudayl, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: When a woman in itikaf menstruates, let her pitch a tent in her house, and when she is pure she makes up those days. Fudayl said: And Mansur told me, from Ibrahim, he said: She puts a curtain in her house."
"7885","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if she becomes pure while in her house, may her husband touch her that day? He said: No, unless that breaks her jiwar. I said: Not even kiss her? He said: No. I said: In her menses may her husband kiss her? He said: Yes. I said: And have intimate contact above? He said: Yes. Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar said it. Ata said: And he may have from her what a man has from his menstruating wife outside jiwar."
"7886","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: She complains of an illness that prevents her fasting? He said: She returns to her house if she wishes until she recovers. I said: May her husband touch her in her illness? He said: No, by Allah, then, unless she breaks her jiwar. I said: Not a kiss, nor anything? He said: No."
"7887","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: There is no harm if the mujawira woman is married in her jiwar. Ibn Jurayj said: And Ata was asked: May the mutakifa woman perfume herself and adorn herself? He said: No, do you want her husband to fall upon her? She should not perfume herself. I said: She did it, does that break her jiwar? He said: No, and why should she do that? She is in worship and humility, the perfume and adornment of a woman is only for her husband."
"7888","From Mamar, it was disliked that the mutakif perfume himself."
"7889","From Malik, he said: There is no harm in perfume for the mutakif."
"7890","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr told me, from Yahya ibn Jada, that a woman went out perfumed in the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he found her scent, so he beat her with the stick, then said: You go out perfumed, and men find your scent, and the hearts of men are at their noses. Go out unscented."
"7891","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: It was forbidden for a woman to perfume herself and adorn herself then go out. I said: And the married one? He said: And the married one. Then he said: And do not display yourselves. Another man said to him: Is that tabarruj? He said: Yes, she goes out like that, so it is asked about her: Who is she?"
"7892","From al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Ubayd, freedman of Abu Ruhm, from Abu Hurayra, he said: A woman whose perfume was spreading, with a whirlwind to her tail, met him, and he said to her: O maidservant of the Almighty, where have you come from? She said: From the mosque. He said: Did you perfume yourself for it? She said: Yes. He said: Go back, for I heard my beloved Abu al-Qasim say: Allah does not accept the prayer of a woman who perfumes herself for this mosque, or for the mosque, until she bathes like her bath from janaba. From Mamar, from Layth, from a man, from Abu Hurayra, similar to it."
"7893","From Mamar, from Layth, that a woman went out adorned, her husband having permitted her, and Umar ibn al-Khattab was told of her, so he sought her but could not find her. Then he stood preaching and said: This is the outgoing woman, and this is her sender. If I could overpower them I would disgrace them. Then he said: A woman goes out to her father struggling with himself and to her brother struggling with himself, so when she goes out let her wear her shabby clothes, and when she returns let her take her adornment in her house, and adorn herself for her husband. Abd al-Razzaq said: He means by shattartu: I would disgrace them, and al-maawiz is worn-out clothes."
"7894","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Ajlan, from Yaqub ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Busr ibn Said, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to the wife of Abdullah ibn Masud: When one of you wants to attend Isha, let her not touch perfume."
"7895","From al-Thawri, from Ubayd ibn Yazid ibn Suraqa, from his mother: That she sent to Hafsa, who was her sister, asking her about perfume, and she wanted to go out. So Hafsa, wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Perfume is only for the bed."
"7896","From al-Thawri, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, from Abu al-Zara, he said: Abdullah ibn Masud said: For me to jostle a camel smeared with tar is dearer to me than to jostle a perfumed woman, and for a mans belly to be filled with pus is better for him than to be filled with poetry. From Mamar, from a man, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, from Abu al-Zara, likewise."
"7897","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from al-Amash, he said: The wife of Ibrahim asked his permission to visit some of her family, and he permitted her. When she went out, he found a pleasant scent from her, so he said: Go back, for when a woman perfumes herself then goes out, it is only fire and disgrace."
"7898","From Ibn Uyayna, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab patrolled the rows of women and found a pleasant scent from the head of a woman, so he said: If I knew which of you she is I would do, and do. Let one of you perfume herself for her husband, and when she goes out let her wear the rags of her slave girl. He said: It reached me that the woman who had perfumed herself urinated in her clothes from fear."
"7899","From Umar ibn Dharr, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: No deed is better than a deed in the ten of Dhu al-Hijja. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, not even jihad in the path of Allah? He said: Not even jihad in the path of Allah, unless it reaches killing."
"7900","Umar said: I mentioned that to Mujahid, and he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: No deed is better than a deed in the ten. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, not even jihad in the path of Allah? He said: Not even jihad in the path of Allah, unless a man goes out with himself and his wealth, and returns with nothing of that."
"7901","Mamar told us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, he said: No deed in the days of the year is better than it in the ten of Dhu al-Hijja. He said: And they are the ten which Allah completed for Musa."
"7902","From Mamar, from al-Amash, from Abu al-Duha, he said: Masruq was asked about: By the dawn, and ten nights. He said: They are the best days of the year."
"7903","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Muslim al-Batin, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: There are no days in which deeds are dearer to Allah, or better, than the ten days. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, not even jihad? He said: Not even jihad, except a man who goes out with himself and his wealth, and returns with nothing of that."
"7904","Mamar told us, from Qatada, he said: Adi ibn Artat said to al-Hasan: Will you not take the people out and make them stand with them? That was in Basra. He said: Al-Hasan said: Standing is only at Arafa. He said: And al-Hasan used to say: The first who made people stand in our land was Ibn Abbas."
"7905","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abu Bakr al-Hudhali, he said: I entered upon al-Hasan while he was praying, and I discussed something of the Quran with his son. Then he turned to us and said: What are you discussing? He said: I said: Ta-Sin, and Ha-Mim. He said: Openings with which the Quran is opened. He said: I said: The freedman of Ibn Abbas said so and so. He said: As soon as he mentioned the freedman of Ibn Abbas, he said: Ibn Abbas was of high rank in Islam. Ibn Abbas was of high rank in the Quran. Umar used to say: That is the youth of the elders, he has an asking tongue and an understanding heart. He used to stand on this pulpit of ours, I think he said: on the evening of Arafa, and recite Surat al-Baqara and Surat Al Imran, explaining them verse by verse, and he was an abundant pouring sea."
"7906","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, he said: I heard al-Hasan say: The first who made people stand in our land was Ibn Abbas. He would promise on the evening of Arafa, and recite the Quran, al-Baqara, verse by verse, and he was a pouring scholar."
"7907","From Malik, from Ziyad ibn Abi Ziyad, from Talha ibn Ubaydullah ibn Kurayz, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The best supplication is the supplication of the day of Arafa, and the best I and the prophets before me have said is the saying: There is no god but Allah alone, no partner for Him."
"7908","Malik said: And Ibrahim ibn Abi Abla told me, from Talha ibn Ubaydullah ibn Kurayz, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: There is no day in which Satan is more driven away, nor more debased, nor more angry, than the day of Arafa, because of what he sees of mercy descending, and Allah overlooking grave matters, except what he saw on the day of Badr. It was said: What did he see on the day of Badr? He said: He saw Jibril, peace be upon him, marshalling the angels."
"7909","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, from Hisham, from al-Hasan, he said: Fasting a day of the ten equals two months."
"7910","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: I was told that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was never seen fasting in the ten at all."
"7911","From Mamar, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: He used to see people making the stand in the mosque in Kufa, and would not stand with them."
"7912","From Mamar, from Qatada, from Anas ibn Malik: That the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to sacrifice in Medina two horned black-and-white rams."
"7913","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad, from Abu Salama, from Aisha, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sacrificed two rams."
"7914","Mamar told us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, he said: Numan ibn Abi Futayma passed by the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, with a horned wide-eyed ram. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: How like this is to the ram which Ibrahim sacrificed. Then Muadh ibn Afra bought a horned wide-eyed ram, and gifted it to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he sacrificed it."
"7915","From al-Aslami, from Dawud, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sacrificed a wide-eyed horned stud ram."
"7916","Mamar told us, he said: It reached me that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, slaughtered at the prayer place, or he said: sacrificed."
"7917","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is sacrifice obligatory upon people? He said: No, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, slaughtered."
"7918","From Mamar, from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that he said to a man: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sacrificed, and if you leave it there is nothing upon you."
"7919","Mamar told us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that he would not sacrifice for a fetus, but he would sacrifice for his young and old children, and make aqiqa for all his children."
"7920","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Hanash, that Ali sacrificed two rams."
"7921","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Ibn Umar, he said: Sacrifices are nothing, or he said: They are not obligatory, whoever wishes sacrifices, and whoever wishes does not sacrifice."
"7922","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: None of his family in Medina would ask him for a sacrifice but he sacrificed for him, and he would not sacrifice for them at Mina."
"7923","From al-Thawri, from Ismail, and Mutarrif, from al-Shabi, from Abu Surayha, he said: I saw Abu Bakr and Umar not sacrificing."
"7924","From Mamar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri: Shall we sacrifice for the absent? He said: There is no harm in it."
"7925","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, that Umar ibn al-Khattab would perform hajj and not sacrifice."
"7926","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: Concession was given to the pilgrim and the traveler not to sacrifice."
"7927","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to perform hajj with silver coins with them and not sacrifice."
"7928","From al-Thawri, from al-Hasan ibn Amr, from Fudayl, from Ibrahim, he said: They, when present, sacrificed, and when traveling, did not sacrifice."
"7929","From al-Thawri, from Kulayb ibn Wail, from his uncle, he said: Sad ibn Malik sent to us while we were at Mina: We did not slaughter, and did not sacrifice, so feed us."
"7930","From al-Thawri, from Abu Mashar, Abu Bakr said, and I heard it from Abu Mashar, from a man, freedman of Ibn Abbas, he said: Ibn Abbas sent me to buy him meat for two dirhams, and said: Say: This is the sacrifice of Ibn Abbas."
"7931","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, he said: Alqama said: For me not to sacrifice is dearer to me than to see it binding upon me."
"7932","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Abu Wail, from Uqba ibn Amr, he said: I thought to leave the sacrifice, though I am among the most able of you in it, fearing it would be thought binding obligatory."
"7933","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, he said: Abu Masud al-Ansari said: I leave the sacrifice, though I am wealthy, fearing my neighbors will see it as binding upon me."
"7934","From al-Thawri, from Bayan, from al-Shabi, from Surayha, or Abu Surayha, Abu Bakr doubted, he said: My family compelled me to stinginess after I knew of the sunna. The household used to sacrifice a sheep and two sheep, and now our neighbors make us miserly."
"7935","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from a man, from Abu Hurayra, he said: There is no harm if a man sacrifices a sheep for his household."
"7936","From al-Thawri, from Khalid, from Ikrima, that Abu Hurayra would slaughter a sheep, his family saying: And for us, and he would say: And for you."
"7937","From al-Aslami, from Abu Jabir al-Bayadi, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Uqba ibn Amir, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, distributed sheep among us, and there fell to me a young goat. So I sacrificed it for my household, then I asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he said: It has sufficed you."
"7938","From al-Aslami, from Yunus ibn Sayf, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: We knew only that, until we mixed with the people of Iraq, meaning: The household used to sacrifice a sheep, then they sacrificed for every one a sheep."
"7939","From al-Thawri, from Salih, from al-Shabi, he said: I performed three pilgrimages, I shed no blood in them. He said: For me to leave it while wealthy is dearer to me than to sacrifice while poor."
"7940","From al-Thawri, from Imran ibn Muslim, from Suwayd ibn Ghafala, he said: I heard Bilal say: I would not care if I sacrificed a rooster, and for me to give its price to an orphan or a dusty poor one is dearer to me than to sacrifice it. He said: I do not know whether Suwayd said it from himself, or it is from the saying of Bilal."
"7941","Mamar told us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: For me to sacrifice a young animal is dearer to me than to sacrifice an old decrepit one. Allah is most deserving of richness and generosity, and the dearest of them to me to sacrifice is the dearest of them to me to keep."
"7942","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, he said: None of you should gift to Allah what he would be ashamed to gift to a noble. Allah is the noblest of the noble, and most deserving to be chosen for."
"7943","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim told me, from Habib ibn Mikhnaf, from his father, he said: I reached the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on the day of Arafa, while he was saying: Do you know it? He said: I do not know what they answered him. He said: Then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Upon every household to slaughter a sheep in every Rajab, and in every Adha a sheep."
"7944","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aisha, he said: She would slaughter for herself a sheep at Mina, and would not slaughter for us."
"7945","Mamar told us, from al-Zuhri, he said: When a man buys his sacrifice and it sickens with him, or illness befalls it, it is permissible."
"7946","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Tawus, he said: No spending a man spends is greater in reward than blood shed on this day, meaning the day of sacrifice, except kin he joins."
"7947","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibrahim ibn Maysara, he said: I heard Tawus say: Silver never went into anything in like amount better than the price of a sacrificial camel."
"7948","From Ibn Uyayna, from Misar, from Abu Damra, from al-Aswad ibn Hilal, he said: I came to Medina with camels of mine, and said: If I entered the mosque. He said: I entered the mosque, and Umar ibn al-Khattab was preaching, saying: O people of Medina, perform hajj and send sacrificial animals, for Allah loves the sacrificial offering. He said: I returned to my camels, and every man was embracing a camel of them. He said: And Umar came and looked at them, and said: These are the camels of a Muhajir man."
"7949","Al-Thawri told us, from Tawba al-Anbari, from Salma, from Abu Hurayra, he said: I heard him say: The blood of a white one is dearer to Allah than the blood of two black ones."
"7950","Al-Aslami told us, from his father, he said: I heard Ibn al-Musayyib say: For me to sacrifice a sheep is dearer to me than to give a hundred dirhams in charity."
"7951","Abu Said al-Shami told us, he said: Ata ibn Abi Rabah narrated to us, from Aisha, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Sacrifice, and make yourselves content with it, for there is no Muslim who faces his sacrifice to the qibla but its blood, its dung, and its wool are present good deeds in his scale on the Day of Resurrection."
"7952","And he used to say: Spend little, you will be rewarded much. The blood, even if it falls in the dust, is in the protection of Allah, until He pays its owner on the Day of Resurrection."
"7953","Abdullah ibn Muharrar told us, from al-Zuhri, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to Aisha or to Fatima: Witness your sacrifice, for you are forgiven at the first drop of its blood."
"7954","From Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash, from Abu Husayn, from Abu Burda, from Abu Musa al-Ashari: That he used to order his daughters to slaughter their sacrifices with their own hands."
"7955","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The muhrim leaves if he wishes."
"7956","Wahb ibn Nafi told us, that he heard Ikrima narrate: That Ibn Abbas ordered him to slaughter a camel, while he was in ihram."
"7957","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, about His saying: Allah will test you with something of the game which your hands reach. He said: Your taking them from their eggs and their chicks, and your spears, what you shot or stabbed."
"7958","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, about His saying: And whoever kills it among you deliberately. He kills it forgetting his ihram, judgment is passed against him."
"7959","From al-Thawri, from Layth, and Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: When he hits it deliberate to its sanctity, deliberate to its killing, no judgment is passed against him, and when he hits it deliberate to it, forgetting its sanctity, judgment is passed against him."
"7960","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, he said: Judgment is passed against him once in the deliberate, then he retracted and said: Judgment is passed against him in the deliberate, the mistake, the forgetfulness, and every time he hits. Ata said: Allah pardoned what is past. He said: In the Jahiliyya, and whoever hits in Islam Allah will not leave him until He takes revenge from him, and with that the expiation. Abd al-Razzaq said: And Ibn Jurayj said it, from Ata."
"7961","Mamar told us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ata, he said: Judgment is passed against one who hits game every time he repeats. He said: And Mujahid said: Judgment is not passed against him except the first time."
"7962","From al-Thawri, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from al-Hakam, he said: Judgment is passed against him in the mistake and the deliberate."
"7963","Mamar told us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Judgment is passed against him in the deliberate, and in the mistake it is sunna. Abu Bakr said: And it is the saying of the people, and we take it."
"7964","Mamar and al-Thawri told us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to say to a man when he hit game in the Haram deliberately: Did you hit before this? If he said: Yes, they did not judge against him, and said: Ask Allah for forgiveness. And if he said: No, they judged against him. From al-Thawri, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Shabi, from Shurayh, like the saying of Ibrahim. Dawud said: I mentioned that to Said ibn Jubayr, and he said: He was judged, shall he strip off."
"7965","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Tawus, he said: Judgment is passed against him in the deliberate, and there is nothing upon him in the mistake. He said: By Allah, Allah said only: And whoever kills it among you deliberately."
"7966","Mamar told us, from Qatada, he said: The deliberate offender is not judged except once, and whoever returns, Allah takes revenge from him."
"7967","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Hakam, that Umar judged in the mistake."
"7968","From Hisham, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, about the muhrim who hits game, then judgment is passed against him, then he returns. He said: No judgment is passed against him. If Allah wills He pardons him, and if He wills He seizes him. He said: And he recited this verse: And whoever returns, Allah takes revenge from him. Hisham said: And al-Hasan said: Judgment is passed against him every time he hits, in the mistake and the deliberate."
"7969","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: A man hit game deliberately in the Haram twice, then fire came and struck him and burned him. Mamar said: And it reached me that a man in the Jahiliyya took a gazelle in the Haram, and held it by its neck until the gazelle urinated. He said: A snake came and coiled on the neck of the man, and kept strangling him until he urinated, then it left him."
"7970","From al-Thawri, from Khusayf, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: Concession was given in killing game once in the Haram, and if he returns Allah will not leave him until He takes revenge from him in the deliberate."
"7971","From Waki, from al-Thawri, he said: Jabir told me, from al-Hakam, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote: That judgment be passed against him every time he hits."
"7972","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Does the imam punish in it? He said: No, a sin he sinned between him and his Lord. Al-Thawri, from his companions: But let him ransom."
"7973","And al-Thawri was asked, while I was listening, about the slave who hits game? He said: He fasts. It was said to him: If his master gives him what to slaughter? He said: Fasting is dearer to me. Then he said: Layth told me, from Mujahid, he said: There is nothing upon the slave except fasting."
"7974","Fudayl told us, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: There is nothing upon the slave except prayer and fasting."
"7975","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, and Qatada, and from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, they said: A man has a choice in fasting, charity, and rite, in the recompense of game."
"7976","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: everything in the Quran with 'or' gives a choice, and everything with 'if you do not find' is to be taken in order, first then next. Sufyan said: he should discharge what became due upon him promptly on that occasion and not delay it."
"7977","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, about His saying 'a recompense like what he killed of livestock, to be judged by two just men among you': he said: two just men judge a sacrificial animal against him; if a sacrificial animal is available, otherwise the sacrificial animal is valued as food, then the food is valued as fasting, a day's fast in place of each poor person's meal. Mujahid said: a day's fast in place of every two mudds."
"7978","From Hisham, from al-Hasan, about a man who struck game and could not find its recompense: he said: it is valued in dirhams, then the dirhams are valued as food, then he fasts two days for every sa'. Ata said: four days for every sa'."
"7979","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, he said: when a pilgrim in ihram strikes game, an equivalent from livestock is judged against him, and it is said to him: buy it; if he cannot find it, the value of that is assessed against him as food; if he cannot find food, they consider how much the food would be, and he fasts a day in place of every half sa'. Mujahid said: if he finds part of the food but not all of it, he fasts; and if he struck an animal whose price is not half a sa', he fasts a day in its place. Al-Thawri said: Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata: if he is well-off he has the choice, if he wishes he fasts, if he wishes he slaughters, and if he wishes he feeds; and he said about 'or its equivalent in fasting': the equivalent of the food is fasting, a mudd for every day."
"7980","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Jurayj: I said to Ata, what is 'or its equivalent in fasting'? He said: if he struck a sheep, the sheep is valued as food, then a mudd is assigned for every day, which he fasts."
"7981","From Ibn Jurayj, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: the least for game is a sheep."
"7982","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from al-Hakam, from Ibn Abbas, he said: food was only made a measure by which the fasting is known."
"7983","From Hushaym, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, about the recompense for game when the pilgrim in ihram cannot find it: he said: he fasts between three and ten days."
"7984","From Ibn Jurayj: al-Walid informed me, from Ibn Umar, that he said: half a sa' for every day. Ibn Jurayj said: and it reached me that Ibn Abbas said the same."
"7985","Ma'mar informed us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: for the ostrich a camel, for the wild donkey a cow, for the wild cow a cow, for the large full-grown ibex a cow, for what is below the ibex a sheep, and for the hyrax a sheep."
"7986","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: as for what has already been judged and the practice has passed, for the ostrich is a full-grown camel."
"7987","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata al-Khurasani, from Ibn Abbas, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, Ali ibn Abi Talib, Uthman ibn Affan and Zayd ibn Thabit said: for the ostrich killed by the pilgrim in ihram, a camel from camels is due."
"7988","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: I asked about the ransom for game? He said: assessment; two just men judge against him at that time. If you look at what was judged in the past, high and low prices break it; rather judgment is passed against him at the time he strikes it. Ma'mar said: al-Hasan said it too."
"7989","Abdullah ibn al-Muharrar informed us: I heard Qatadah narrating: Abu al-Malih ibn Usamah wrote to Abu Ubaydah ibn Abdullah asking him about the ostrich struck by the pilgrim in ihram, so Abu Ubaydah wrote to him: in it is a camel."
"7990","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: for the wild donkey a cow; Ibn Jurayj reported it from Ata too."
"7991","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: for the wild cow a cow."
"7992","From Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: for the wild cow a cow; Ibn Jurayj reported it from Ata too."
"7993","From Isra'il or another, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: for the wild cow a cow."
"7994","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: for the large full-grown ibex a cow, and for what is below that of ibex a ram."
"7995","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: for the ibex a cow."
"7996","From Ibn Jurayj: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, from his father, he said: for a gazelle-like sheep of the antelopes a sheep, and the least for game is a sheep."
"7997","From Abdullah ibn Muharrar: I heard Qatadah saying: Abu Malih ibn Usamah wrote to Abu Ubaydah ibn Abdullah asking him about the wild donkey struck by the pilgrim in ihram, so he wrote to him: in it is a camel, or he said: a cow."
"7998","From Ma'mar and Malik, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that Umar ibn al-Khattab judged a sheep for the gazelle."
"7999","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: for the gazelle a sheep."
"8000","From Malik and Ma'mar, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that Umar ibn al-Khattab judged a young weaned goat for the jerboa. Ma'mar said: al-Zuhri said: an assessment."
"8001","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Abu Ubaydah ibn Abdullah, that Ibn Mas'ud said about a man who threw a sack on a jerboa and killed it while in ihram: he judged in it a young goat."
"8002","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: for every buck-toothed animal a sheep, and for the jerboa a sheep."
"8003","From Ibn Jurayj: Abu Shaddad informed me: I heard Mujahid saying: for the jerboa a kid. Ibn Jurayj said: so I asked Ata, and he said: I have not heard anything about it."
"8004","From Ma'mar, from Sulayman al-A'mash, from Sulayman ibn Maysarah, from Tariq ibn Shihab, he said: we set out as pilgrims, and there were snakes as if boiling pots, and we killed them. And a man among us ran his camel over a lizard and crushed its back. So I asked Umar ibn al-Khattab about the snakes, and he said: you killed an enemy. And we asked him about the lizard, so he turned to me and to a man and said: do you see a kid that has reached water and trees sufficing for it? He said: yes, so he ordered him with it."
"8005","From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Mukhariq ibn Abdullah: I heard Tariq ibn Shihab saying: we set out as pilgrims, and a man among us called Arbad ibn Abdullah ran over a lizard. So we came to ask Umar ibn al-Khattab, and Arbad asked him. Umar said to him: judge in it. He said: you are better and more knowledgeable than me. He said: I only ordered you to judge. He said: I said: in it is a kid that has joined water and trees. He said: then in it is that. He said: and we struck snakes in the sand while in ihram, so we asked Ibn Umar about them, and he said: they are an enemy, kill them wherever you find them."
"8006","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: for the lizard a handful of food, because the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not eat it. Abd al-Razzaq said: a handful, meaning a fill of the palm."
"8007","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, that Ali deemed the hyena game and judged in it a ram."
"8008","From Ma'mar and Malik, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that Umar judged a ram for the hyena, a sheep for the gazelle, a young she-goat for the hare, and a young weaned goat for the jerboa."
"8009","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that he heard Ibn Abbas saying: for the hyena a ram."
"8010","Ibn Jurayj said: Muhammad informed me that he heard Ikrimah, the freedman of Ibn Abbas, saying about the hyena: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ranked it as game, and judged in it a Najdi ram."
"8011","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, he said: if I had a judgment with me I would judge a kid for the fox. Ma'mar said: so I mentioned that to Ibn Abi Najih, and he said: we only counted it a beast of prey, so I see he has made it game."
"8012","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: for the fox a sheep."
"8013","From Hushaym: al-Hajjaj informed us, from Ata, he said: for the fox a lamb."
"8014","From Ibn Jurayj: Amr ibn Dinar said: we never heard that ransom is paid for the fox."
"8015","From Isra'il, from Simak ibn Harb, from al-Nu'man ibn Humayd Abu Qudamah, from Umar ibn al-Khattab that he judged a kid or a young she-goat for the hare."
"8016","From Ma'mar and Malik, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that Umar judged a young she-goat for the hare."
"8017","From Humayd, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Abd al-Malik Abu al-Mughirah, from Abdullah ibn al-Miqdam, from Amr ibn Habashi that he and Ibn Abbas judged a young strong goat or a weaned kid for the hare."
"8018","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: for the hyrax a sheep."
"8019","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: for the hare a sheep."
"8020","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: for the hyrax a sheep."
"8021","From Ibn Jurayj: Ata said: for the hyrax, if it is eaten, a sheep."
"8022","From Isra'il, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah: that a man struck a gazelle while in ihram, so he came to Ali and asked him, and he said: offer a ram from sheep."
"8023","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr: Qabisah ibn Jabir al-Asadi informed me, he said: I was in ihram, and I saw a gazelle and shot it, and I hit the base of its horn, and it mounted its fright and fell to it. Something of that entered my heart, so I came to Umar ibn al-Khattab to ask him. When I came to him I found a fair man with a thin face, and he was Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf. He said: so I asked Umar, and he turned to Abd al-Rahman and said: do you see a sheep sufficing him? He said: yes, so he ordered me to slaughter a sheep. We stood up from him, and a companion of mine said: the Commander of the Believers did not do well in giving you fatwa until he asked the man. Umar heard his speech, so Umar beat him with the stick, then Umar turned to me to beat me, so I said: Commander of the Believers, I said nothing, it was only he who said it. He said: so he left me. Then he said: you meant to kill what is inviolable and to overstep the fatwa. Man has ten traits, he said: nine good and one bad, and that bad one corrupts them. And he said: beware the slip of youth."
"8024","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Qabisah ibn Jabir al-Asadi, he said: we set out as pilgrims, and as we were travelling the people's dispute grew: which is swifter in pace, the gazelle or the horse? When a gazelle appeared to us from the left toward the right, and a man among us shot it and did not miss the base of its horn, and it mounted its fright. He was confounded, until we came to Umar, and we came to him while he was at Mina. I sat before him, I and he, and he told him the story. He said: how did you strike it, by mistake or on purpose? Sufyan said: Mis'ar said: I meant to shoot it but I did not mean to kill it. He said: and I remember he said: so the man got confused and said: I struck it neither by mistake nor on purpose. Mis'ar said: so he said to him: you have combined purpose and mistake. He said: so he leaned to a man whose face was as if a heart, and consulted him privately, then he turned to us and said: take a sheep, spill its blood, give its meat in charity, and make its skin a waterskin. He said: so we stood up from him, and I said: O you who ask fatwa of Ibn al-Khattab, his fatwa will not avail you anything before Allah, so slaughter your she-camel and magnify the rites of Allah. By Allah, Umar did not know until he asked the man beside him. So the bright-eyed one went and reported it to Umar. By Allah, I did not realize but that he came to my companion with the stick, beating him, then he said: may Allah fight you, do you overstep the fatwa and kill what is inviolable? He said: then he turned to me, and I said: Commander of the Believers, I do not permit for you anything Allah has forbidden to you. He said: so he took hold of my clothes and said: I see you a man eloquent of tongue, broad of chest, and a man may have ten traits, nine righteous and one bad, and the bad trait corrupts the nine righteous. Fear the slip of youth."
"8025","From Hushaym, from Mansur or another, from Ibn Sirin: that two pilgrims in ihram raced to the pass of al-Batin, and one of them struck a gazelle and killed it. So he came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he said: slaughter a dusky sheep."
"8026","Ma'mar informed us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, that Maymunah or Umm al-Fadl, Abu Bakr doubted, locked the door of her house upon a cat in Mecca and its two kittens, and she went out to Mina and Arafah, and found them dead. She mentioned that to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, so he ordered her to free a slave for each one of them."
"8027","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata: Ibn Abbas was asked about hunting locusts in the sanctuary, and he forbade it. Either I said, or a man among the people said: your people take them while they sit in the mosque. He said: they do not know."
"8028","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Jurayj: Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj informed me, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, he said: I was with Ibn Abbas when a man asked him about a locust he killed while in ihram? He said: in it is a handful of wheat, though you take a handful of locusts."
"8029","From al-Thawri, from al-Ala ibn al-Musayyab, from a man: I asked Sa'id ibn Jubayr: I killed locusts whose number I do not know while in ihram. He said: take dates whose number you do not know, and give them in charity."
"8030","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul: that Umar ibn al-Khattab was asked about the locust killed by the pilgrim in ihram, and he said: a date is better than a locust."
"8031","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad that Ka'b asked: Commander of the Believers, while we were lighting a fire a locust I threw it into the fire while in ihram, and I gave a dirham in charity. Umar said: you people of Hims, your leaves are many; a date is more beloved to me than your locust."
"8032","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: for the locust a handful or a morsel."
"8033","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from al-Walid ibn Abdullah, he said: I saw Sa'id ibn Jubayr in Mecca going out and seeing locusts in the boys' hands, and killing them from their hands; and he deemed it game."
"8034","From al-Aslami, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: the least the pilgrim in ihram strikes is the locust, and in what is below it there is no recompense, and in it is a date."
"8035","From al-Aslami: Zayd ibn Aslam informed me that Umar judged a date for the locust."
"8036","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he said about the louse killed by the pilgrim in ihram, does it have a recompense? He said: there is nothing in it."
"8037","From Hushaym, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: it has no recompense."
"8038","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, about the louse and the ant and the like of small creatures, when the pilgrim in ihram kills them: a handful of food."
"8039","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from Ata, he said: if the pilgrim in ihram kills it, in it is a handful of food."
"8040","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: for the louse a handful or a morsel; if you killed it without realizing, there is nothing upon you. I said: is the locust like it? He said: like it."
"8041","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: you kill the louse while in Mecca when not in ihram, and you take it while in ihram and throw it away if you see it on your garment or skin, but do not kill it; and delousing, no; and do not kill it otherwise while in ihram."
"8042","From Ma'mar, from Simak ibn al-Fadl: Ibn al-Baylamani narrated to me, he said: I was with Ibn Umar while he leaned on me, when a man came and said: what do you say about a pilgrim in ihram who killed a louse? Ibn Umar said: he slaughters a camel. He said: so I laughed, and he looked at me and said: do not blame me; by Allah, by my life, he asks me about the louse while one of them leaps upon his brother with the sword."
"8043","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Ata, from A'ishah, she said: the pilgrim in ihram kills all pests except the louse, for it is from him."
"8044","From al-Thawri, from Hammad: I asked Sa'id ibn Jubayr about the louse killed by the pilgrim in ihram, and he said: everything you fed on its behalf is better than it."
"8045","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah: a man came to Ibn Umar and asked him about the pilgrim in ihram killing a louse. Ibn Umar said: the people of Iraq ask me about the louse, while they killed Husayn ibn Fatimah."
"8046","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Mijlaz: that Ibn Umar was asked about the pilgrim in ihram killing the louse? He said: does one of you kill his Muslim brother while he asks about the louse? Then a woman came to him and said: she killed a louse while in ihram, so what is its expiation? Ibn Umar said: we do not know the louse to be of game. She repeated, and he said the same. She repeated to him a third time, and he said: a sheep is better than a louse. And he looked at me so I would testify with him, so I said: yes, a sheep is better than a louse."
"8047","From Abdullah ibn Muharrar: I heard Maymun ibn Mihran narrating that he heard a man asking Ibn Abbas: I threw a louse in Mecca while in ihram, without remembering, then I sought it and did not find it. Ibn Abbas said: that stray is not to be sought."
"8048","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Humayd came to Ibn Abbas and said: my son killed a dove in Mecca. Ibn Abbas said: seek a sheep and give it in charity. Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Amr, from Ata the same."
"8049","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Qays ibn Sa'd, from Ata, that Umar and Ibn Abbas judged a sheep for the dove of Mecca."
"8050","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, that Umar passed by a dove and it flew and fell upon al-Marwah, and a snake seized it and killed it. So Umar assigned in it a sheep."
"8051","From Ma'mar, from Jabir, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, that a dove was upon the House and dropped dung upon Umar's hand, so Umar gestured with his hand and it flew and fell in one of the houses of Mecca, and a snake came and ate it. So Umar made its recompense a sheep."
"8052","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: for the dove of the sanctuary a sheep, and for the dove outside the sanctuary a dirham."
"8053","From al-Thawri, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: for the dove a sheep."
"8054","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, and al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, they said: for the dove its price."
"8055","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id: I heard Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab saying: whoever strikes a dove of the doves of Mecca, upon him is a sheep."
"8056","From Hushaym: Abu Bishr ibn Abi Wahshiyah narrated to me, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, and from Yusuf ibn Mahak: that a man locked his door upon a dove and its two chicks, then set out to Mina and Arafat, and returned and they had died. He said: so he came to Ibn Umar and mentioned that to him, and he assigned upon him three sheep, and appointed a man with him to judge."
"8057","From al-Thawri, he said: for the dove chick a kid."
"8058","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: for the Syrian dove its price, nothing more is upon you in it."
"8059","From Ma'mar: Sadaqah ibn Yasar informed me that he asked Salim and al-Qasim ibn Muhammad about a partridge he slaughtered while in Mecca forgetfully. One of them said to his companion: is a partridge in a man's belly better or two-thirds of a mudd? He said: rather two-thirds of a mudd. He said: is it better or half a mudd? He said: half a mudd. He said: is it better or a third of a mudd? He said: I said to them: does a sheep suffice me? They said: do you know? He said: yes. They said: then go. From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Sadaqah ibn Yasar the same, except he said: a sandgrouse in place of partridge, and he did not say partridge."
"8060","From Ma'mar, from Sadaqah ibn Yasar: I asked Sa'id ibn Jubayr about a partridge I slaughtered while not in ihram in Mecca, and he saw no harm upon me. He said: how do you buy it? He said: twenty for a dirham. He said: I will guide you to one who sells it forty for a dirham."
"8061","From Abdullah ibn Muharrar, that he heard Ata saying: for the small birds, a mudd each, meaning the vulture and the like."
"8062","From Ibn Jurayj: Ata said to me: the hoopoe is below the dove and above the sparrow; in it is a dirham. As for the small lark it is a sparrow. As for the bat it is above the sparrow and below the hoopoe, so in it is two-thirds of a dirham. Whatever bird does not reach being a dove and is above the sparrow, in it is a dirham."
"8063","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: for the small wild bird or its like, the turtle-dove, the sandgrouse, the bustard, the collared dove and the partridge, a sheep each. Abd al-Razzaq said: as for Ibn Jurayj, he mentioned from Ata that he said: for every bird of dove size and above, a sheep each, collared or turtle-dove, partridge, sandgrouse and bustard, meaning the sparrow, the stone-curlew, the crane, the water-bird and the like of birds among them, a sheep. I said: did you hear it? He said: no, except about the dove."
"8064","From Ibn Jurayj: Abd al-Karim informed me that Abu al-Khalil informed him that a man came to Ibn Abbas and said: I struck a quail while forbidden in ihram. So Ibn Abbas judged upon him a sheep."
"8065","Ibn Jurayj said: Ata said: for the sparrow half a dirham."
"8066","From Amr ibn Qays, from Ata, that Uthman ibn Affan set out as pilgrim and locked the door upon doves, and found them dead. So he judged for every dove a sheep."
"8067","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, from Ata, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, and he was asked about a pilgrim in ihram who struck a dove of the doves of the sanctuary: he said: two just men among you judge it. He said: a sheep, then for every egg a dirham is judged."
"8068","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, he said: for an egg of the eggs of the doves of Mecca half a dirham; if it was broken and in it a chick, in it is a dirham."
"8069","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: I asked about the egg of the dove struck by the pilgrim in ihram, and he said: judgment is passed upon him when he strikes it, its price."
"8070","From Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: for an egg of the eggs of doves outside the sanctuary a mudd."
"8071","From a man called Abu Shayban: a shaykh of Basra called Ibn Hurmuz informed me, he said: I stepped upon a nest of the doves of Mecca while in Mecca, in it a fledged chick and an egg. So I killed the chick and broke the egg. I asked Ata, and he said about the dead: a sheep, but go to that circle, for in it is a shaykh, Ubayd ibn Umayr, so ask him; if he informs you of something, return to me and inform me. So I asked Ubayd, and he said: as for the fledged chick, in it is a sheep; as for the egg, in it is half a dirham. I said to him: what do I do? He said: slaughter the sheep, and buy food with half a dirham, grind it, and look at the poor near you and feed them. If you are strangers or needy, keep some of it. I passed by Ata and informed him, and he said: thus Ibn Abbas informed me."
"8072","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, and from Ata, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, he said: for two eggs a dirham."
"8073","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: I asked him about the egg of the ostrich struck by the pilgrim in ihram, and he said: assessment; judgment is passed upon him when he strikes it, its price."
"8074","From Ma'mar, from Matar al-Warraq, from Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah: that a man of the Ansar trampled the nest of an ostrich while in ihram, meaning its nest, and broke an egg. So he asked Ali, and he said: upon you is a young she-camel, or he said: a stallion camel. The Ansari went out and came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and informed him. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I have heard what Ali said, but come to the concession: fasting or feeding a poor person."
"8075","From Abdullah ibn Muharrir, from Qatadah: Abu Mulayh ibn Usamah wrote to Abu Ubaydah ibn Abdullah asking him about the egg of the ostrich struck by the pilgrim in ihram. Abu Ubaydah wrote to him: that Abdullah ibn Mas'ud used to say: in it is a day's fast or feeding a poor person."
"8076","He said: and I heard Qatadah narrating, from Abdullah ibn Husayn, from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, that he said: in it is a day's fast or feeding a poor person. Abdullah ibn Muharrir said: and I heard Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah narrating, from a man of the Ansar, the same."
"8077","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: for the egg of the ostrich struck by the pilgrim in ihram, its price."
"8078","Al-Thawri informed us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, and from Dawud, from al-Sha'bi, they said: in it is its price."
"8079","From Isma'il ibn Abdullah, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, that Umar ibn al-Khattab judged its value for the egg of the ostrich struck by the pilgrim in ihram. Abd al-Razzaq said: so I narrated it to Abu Sufyan, and he said: I heard al-Thawri asking al-A'mash about this hadith, and he narrated it from Umar. Al-Thawri kept repeating it to him, but al-A'mash insisted on affirming it from Umar."
"8080","From Isma'il: I heard al-A'mash narrating, from Ibrahim, he said: for the egg of the ostrich struck by the pilgrim in ihram, and its likes, its value."
"8081","From Abu Khalid: Abu Umayyah al-Thaqafi informed me that Nafi', the freedman of Ibn Umar, informed him, from Aslam, the freedman of Umar, that a man asked Umar about the egg of the ostrich struck by the pilgrim in ihram. Umar said to him: do you see Ali, then ask him, for we have been ordered to consult him."
"8082","From Hushaym, from Khalid, from Ibn Sirin, he said: he judged about one in ihram who pointed one not in ihram to ostrich eggs: he judged in it a day's fast or feeding a poor person."
"8083","Ma'mar informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Jubayr: Ikrimah informed me, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Ali judged about the egg of the ostrich struck by the pilgrim in ihram: you send the stallion upon your camels, and when their pregnancy is clear you name the number of eggs you struck and say: this is a sacrificial offering; then there is no guarantee upon you for what spoils. Ibn Abbas said: Mu'awiyah marvelled at Ali's judgment. Ibn Abbas said: does Mu'awiyah marvel at a wonder? It is only what the eggs were sold for in the market, given in charity."
"8084","From Ibn Jurayj: Ata said: if you have no camels, then for every egg two dirhams. Ata said: whoever has camels, then in it is as Ali said."
"8085","From al-Aslami, from Husayn ibn Abdullah, from Ikrimah, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged its price for the egg of the ostrich struck by the pilgrim in ihram."
"8086","From Abu Hanifah, from Khusayf, from Abu Ubaydah ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: for the egg of the ostrich struck by the pilgrim in ihram, its value."
"8087","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: all you hunted while not in ihram, and what was hunted while you are in ihram, do not eat it."
"8088","From Ibn Jurayj, that Ata informed him, that Ibn Abbas used to forbid eating game when it was brought into the sanctuary alive. Ibn Jurayj said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah asked about it, and he said: had it been slaughtered outside the sanctuary, it would be more beloved to me."
"8089","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, and from Sadaqah ibn Yasar, from Mujahid, he said: there is no harm in the meat of game being eaten in the sanctuary. He said: but game is not slaughtered in the sanctuary; had it been slaughtered outside the sanctuary then brought into the sanctuary slaughtered, there would be no harm in it."
"8090","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: there is no harm if game enters the sanctuary then is slaughtered."
"8091","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata, he said: when game is brought into the sanctuary alive, there is no harm in eating it. It was said to Amr: Ata has withdrawn from this saying. He said: my knowledge of him is that he eats it. So Amr saw no harm in eating it."
"8092","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, and Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, that he disliked it."
"8093","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Abdullah ibn Amir gifted slaughtered gazelles to Ibn Umar while he was in Mecca, and he did not accept them. From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar the same, and he added: and he disliked eating them."
"8094","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that Ibn Amir gifted live gazelles to Ibn Umar, and he returned them and said: would that he had slaughtered them before they entered the sanctuary; when they entered their safe haven, the sanctuary, I have no need of his gift. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata the same."
"8095","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar that he used to dislike the pilgrim in ihram eating of the meat of game in every case. From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar the same."
"8096","From al-Thawri, from Sadaqah ibn Yasar: Ibn Umar used to dislike eating game, even if it was brought into Mecca slaughtered."
"8097","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that Ibn Umar used to deem tame birds and gazelles as game."
"8098","Ma'mar informed us, from Salih ibn Kaysan: I saw game sold alive in Mecca during the rule of Ibn al-Zubayr."
"8099","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi': Ibn Umar disliked the pilgrim in ihram buying game outside the sanctuary then slaughtering it in the sanctuary."
"8100","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: he used to dislike the pilgrim in ihram eating game in every case."
"8101","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: when game is brought into the sanctuary, it is not slaughtered."
"8102","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththamah, he said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed by me while I was at al-Abwa, and I gifted him a wild donkey. He returned it to me, and when he saw dislike in my face he said: it is not our returning to you, but we are in ihram."
"8103","Ibn Jurayj informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muslim informed me, from Tawus, he said: Zayd ibn Arqam came, and Ibn Abbas would ask him to recall: how did you inform me about meat gifted to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while forbidden in ihram? He said: yes, a limb of game meat was gifted to him and he returned it to him, and said: we do not eat it, we are in ihram."
"8104","From al-Thawri, from Qays ibn Muslim, from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali, from A'ishah, she said: a piece of gazelle meat was gifted to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he was in ihram, and he did not eat it. From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, from Qays ibn Muslim, from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad, from A'ishah the same."
"8105","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: I asked A'ishah about game meat for the pilgrim in ihram, and she said: nephew, they are only a few near days that I saw; so what pricks away from your certainty, leave it."
"8106","From Ma'mar, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad: I heard Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal narrating: that Ali disliked game meat while in ihram, and recited this verse: lawful to you is the game of the sea and its food, a provision for you and for travellers, and forbidden to you is the game of the land while you are in ihram."
"8107","From al-Muthanna ibn Jurayj: Yusuf ibn Mahak informed me that he heard Abdullah ibn Amir informing: that Mu'adh ibn Jabal forbade them eating game meat while in ihram."
"8108","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas that he used to dislike game meat for the pilgrim in ihram. He said: I know Ibn Tawus only informed me, from his father, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, disliked it."
"8109","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: it is unrestricted in His saying: and forbidden to you is the game of the land while you are in ihram."
"8110","From al-Muthanna, that he heard Tawus asked about a people in ihram who passed by a people not in ihram who had taken a hyena and they ate of it with them. Tawus said: glory be to Allah. The one asking him about them said: what do they slaughter, a sheep each? Tawus said: yes, if they volunteer; otherwise a sheep suffices them all each day."
"8111","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: when the pilgrim in ihram strikes game, upon him is a ransom; if he ate it, upon him is to give in charity the like of what he ate."
"8112","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il, from al-Sha'bi, he said: there is disagreement about it, and not eating it is better than eating it. Sufyan adopted it. He said: and those who permit it say: it is like the Meccan who does not hunt it in the sanctuary, but when it is brought to him from outside the sanctuary he eats."
"8113","Al-Muthanna informed us, from Ata, he said: when the pilgrim in ihram is compelled to game, he hunts and there is no recompense upon him; and when he finds carrion, he begins with the carrion and leaves the game."
"8114","Al-Thawri was asked, while I listened, about the pilgrim in ihram compelled, who finds carrion, pork and game meat: which does he eat? He said: he eats the pork and the carrion."
"8115","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir: a shaykh called Rabi'ah ibn Abdullah ibn al-Hudayr informed me that Talhah ibn Ubaydullah asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: does the pilgrim in ihram eat game meat when it was slaughtered outside the sanctuary? He said: yes."
"8116","Ma'mar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, he said: I went out with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, at the time of al-Hudaybiyah, and his companions entered ihram while I did not enter ihram. He said: I saw a wild donkey, so I charged it and hunted it. I mentioned its matter to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and mentioned that I had not entered ihram, and that I hunted it only for him. So he ordered his companions to eat, and did not eat of it when I informed him I had hunted it for him."
"8117","From Ibn Uyaynah: Salih ibn Kaysan informed me, from Abu Muhammad the freedman of the Ansar, from Abu Qatadah, he said: I went out with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, until when we were at the sanctuary, some of us in ihram and some not in ihram. I saw people gazing at something. He said: I said: what are you looking at? They were silent to me. So I looked, and I saw a wild donkey. I saddled my horse and took the spear and the whip, then I mounted. The whip fell from me where I mounted, so I said to them: hand it to me. They said: we will not help you upon it with anything. He said: so I took it and seized it, then I came to it from behind a hill and stabbed it, or he said: hamstrung it. He said: some said: its eating is not lawful, and some said: its eating is lawful. He said: I came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, or he said: so we came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he was ahead of us, and he said: eat it, for it is lawful."
"8118","From Malik, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Isa ibn Talhah, from Umayr ibn Salamah al-Damri, from al-Bahzi, he said: when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was at Sifah al-Rawha or near al-Rawha, he came upon a hamstrung wild donkey for the people. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: this has been struck by a man, and he is about to come to it. Then al-Bahzi came to him and said: Messenger of Allah, I hunted this donkey, so dispose of it. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered Abu Bakr to divide it among the companies, while we were in ihram. He said: then we set out until when we were at Uthayat al-Araj, we came upon a gazelle sleeping curled. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered Abu Bakr to stand by it until the people passed it."
"8119","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from his father, from a man of Banu Damrah, he said: when I came for the journey of al-Jar, Umar set out as pilgrim or performer of umrah, and said: come with us, we pass by al-Jar and look at the ships, and praise Allah who moves them. Al-Damri said: travelling singled me out with him among seven men, and night sheltered us at a bedouin's tent. He said: there was a pot bubbling, meaning boiling. Umar said: is there food? They said: no, except gazelle meat we struck yesterday. He said: so they brought it near, and he ate while in ihram."
"8120","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, he said: Ka'b asked Umar ibn al-Khattab about game meat brought to him. He said: I think he said: a wild donkey struck by a man not in ihram while they were in ihram. He said: so we ate of it. Umar said: had you left it, I would have deemed you understand nothing."
"8121","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, that he heard Abu Hurayrah narrating to his father, he said: Some people in ihram asked me about some people not in ihram who had gifted them game, so I ordered them to eat it, then I saw Umar and asked him. He said: How did you rule for them? I told him, and he said: If you had ruled for them otherwise, I would have hurt you. Ma'mar said: And I heard Amr ibn Dinar reporting from Talq ibn Habib that Abu Hurayrah informed Ibn Umar of this report, so Abu Mijlaz said to Ibn Umar: What do you say? He said: What can I say about it, when Umar is better than me and Abu Hurayrah is better than me. Amr said: Ibn Umar used to dislike eating it."
"8122","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Talq ibn Habib, from Faz'ah, he said: A man asked Ibn Umar whether he may eat game meat while in ihram. Ibn Umar then informed him of the view of Umar and Abu Hurayrah, and said: Umar is better than me and Abu Hurayrah is better than me. Amr said: Ibn Umar would not eat it. Amr said: A man accompanied Ibn Umar and ate game meat while in ihram, as if he rebuked him; then when Ibn Umar's food was brought, the man began eating it, so Ibn Umar said: You already had in that (game) what sufficed you instead of this."
"8123","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah: That a man from the people of Sham asked him about game meat he had obtained while in ihram, and he ordered him to eat it. He said: Then I met Umar and told him about the man's question. He said to him: What did you rule for him? I said: To eat it. He said: By Him in Whose Hand is the soul of Umar, if you had ruled for him otherwise, I would have struck you with the stick."
"8124","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib, from his father, that he: Performed umrah with Uthman in a party, and when they were at al-Rawha, bird meat was presented to them. Uthman said: Eat, but he disliked eating any of it. Amr ibn al-As said: Do we eat that which you yourself do not eat? He said: I am not like you in this; it was hunted for me and killed in my name, or he said: on my account."
"8125","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib, from his father, that Uthman disliked eating partridges that had been hunted for them while they were in ihram. He said: It was hunted for me and killed in my name."
"8126","From Ma'mar, and Ibn Uyaynah, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal saying: I was with Uthman between Makkah and Madinah while we were in ihram, and game was hunted for him, so he ordered his companions to eat, but he himself did not eat. He said: It was hunted, or killed, in my name. He said: Then Ali stood up, and it was said to Uthman that he disliked eating it, so he sent for him. Ali said: And forbidden to you is land game while you are in ihram. Amr said to him: Dust in your mouth. Ali said to him: Rather, dust in your mouth."
"8127","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: Al-Zubayr said: We used to take as provision dried strips of wild game while we were in ihram."
"8128","From al-Aslami, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah ibn Hantab, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Land game is lawful for you while you are in ihram, except what you yourselves hunted or was hunted for you."
"8129","From Malik, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, that Ka'b al-Ahbar came from Sham in a party in ihram, until when they were on part of the road they found game meat, and Ka'b ruled for them to eat it. When they came to Umar they mentioned that to him. He said: Who ruled for you with this? They said: Ka'b. He said: I have put him in charge over you until you return. Then when they were on part of the road, the Makkah road, a swarm of locusts passed, and Ka'b ordered them to take and eat. When they came to Umar they mentioned that to him. He said: What led you to rule for them with this? He said: It is sea game. He said: And what makes you know? He said: O Commander of the Believers, by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, it is only what the whale ejects, which it ejects twice every year."
"8130","Al-Thawri was asked about a man who pointed to game while in ihram, or while in the Haram, and another man struck it. He said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, and Ibn Abi Layla, from Ata, that he said: Upon them both is one expiation."
"8131","Al-Thawri said, and Salim al-Aftas informed me, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: The enticer, the one who rouses it, the commander, the guide, the pointer, and the killer are the same; upon each person among them is an expiation, an expiation."
"8132","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, about people who jointly hunt game while in ihram, he said: Upon them is one expiation."
"8133","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, and al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: Upon each person among them is an expiation, just as if they had killed a man, upon each person among them would be a neck (freeing a slave). He said: ... and al-Thawri, and Ash'ath informed me, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, the like of al-Hasan's view; from Ma'mar, from Ja'far ibn Burqan, or another, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim the like of it."
"8134","From Humayd ibn Ruwayman, a man from the people of Sham, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Abd al-Malik ibn al-Mughirah al-Ta'ifi, from Abdullah ibn al-Miqdam, from Amr ibn al-Habashi, he said: I was with Ibn Abbas when a woman came and said: I pointed to a hare and the hireling shot it. Ibn Abbas said: Two just men among you shall judge in it. He said: So I said to the woman: Say: Judge you yourself. She said it to him, and he said: There must be another with me. I said to her: Tell him to choose whom he wishes. He placed his hand upon me and said: Who is this? I said: Amr ibn Habashi. He said: Give us a ruling about an animal that grazes trees and drinks water, in a belly that has not yet cut teeth. He said: So I said: In our view that is the weaned one, the yearling, and the jadha'ah. He said to her: Choose among these if you wish. She said: I find more than that. He said: Then state whatever you wish."
"8135","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Shubrumah, he said: I asked Amir al-Sha'bi about a man who pointed to game while in ihram and then killed it. He said: Upon each one of them is a just equivalent. He said: I said to him: But Hammad said that one expiation suffices them both. He said: By Allah? He said: I said: Yes. He said: If he indeed said it, he has gone mad. He said: Then I came to al-Harith al-Ukli and told him of their two reports, and he was the dearest of the people to me to agree with me, but he said to me: The word is the word of Amir. Do you not see that if a group kills a man, upon each one of them is an expiation? He said: I said: This word is the word of Hammad. Do you not see that upon them is one blood-money?"
"8136","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Uthman ibn Matar, from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Ammar, freedman of Banu Hashim, that he was among people who struck a hyena while in ihram. He said: So we came to Ibn Umar and asked him, and he said: Upon you is one ram. A man among us said: A ram upon each man. Ibn Umar said: He is emboldened by you; one ram is upon you all."
"8137","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I asked Marwan ibn al-Hakam, while we were in Wadi al-Azraq, about things we find in the Quran that have no like, which the muhrim kills. He said: Look at its value and send it to the Ka'bah."
"8138","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Beware of game while you are sacred; do not pursue it nor guide to it, but if you need it for your hajj, slaughter it before entering ihram."
"8139","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I said to Ata: A man ordered me to (get) game and I slaughtered it, and he laughed and said: Enough for you, you have incurred its compensation. I said: I bought game while in ihram and did not slaughter it until I exited ihram, then when I exited I slaughtered it, and he said: No harm. I said to Ata: I bought game while halal and did not slaughter it until I entered ihram? He said: You have incurred it. He said: And if you bought it in ihram and slaughtered it in ihram, you have incurred it also. I said: I bought game while in ihram and kept it with me, then it died. He said: Then you compensate for it. I said to Ata: I bought it while in ihram and gifted it to halal people, and they slaughtered it during my ihram? He said: You compensate for it. He said: I said: But they did not slaughter it until I exited ihram. He said: Its compensation is upon you."
"8140","And I asked al-Thawri about the muhrim who slaughters game, is it lawful for others to eat it? He said: Layth informed me, from Ata, that he said: It is not lawful for anyone to eat it. Al-Thawri said: And Ash'ath informed me, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, that he said: There is no harm in eating it. Al-Thawri said: And the view of al-Hakam is dearer to me."
"8141","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If he slaughtered it and then ate it, then two expiations."
"8142","From Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, from Rabi'ah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that he asked al-Qasim and Salim about it, and they said: It is not lawful for anyone to eat it."
"8143","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you wait over game after rescuing it from the talons of the falcon or the dog, then it dies, do not eat it."
"8144","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If the muhrim shoots game and does not know what became of the game, let him compensate for it."
"8145","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said: I shot game and hit its vital spot, then found in it a flicker of life but missed its slaughter. He said: Then do not eat it. And from Ata, he said: If a man takes game then releases it and does not know what became of it, let him give something in charity."
"8146","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, he said: Do not shoot game while you are in the hill (outside the Haram) and it is in the Haram; if you do, you compensate. And do not eat game you shot and hit after it had entered the Haram before you seized it."
"8147","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: If you shoot game in the hill and it enters the Haram and dies in it, do not eat it, and there is no compensation upon you for it."
"8148","From al-Thawri, he said: And if you shoot game in the hill and hit it, then wait until it enters the Haram and perishes in it, do not eat it, and there is nothing upon you. He said: And they say about the dog sent in the hill that crosses until it catches in the Haram: There is nothing upon him. Al-Thawri said: No, except from Ata."
"8149","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: He disliked that a man send his dogs while he is in the Haram upon game in the hill; if he does and they kill, upon him is its full compensation. Ata said: And if you release your dogs in the hill and they kill in the Haram, there is no compensation upon you, but do not eat it. I said to him: But I seized it in the hill then entered the Haram and caught it alive? He said: Leave it; it is not yours. He said: I killed it in the Haram? He said: It is not yours; do not eat it either."
"8150","From Humayd, from al-Hajjaj, from Uthman ibn Abdullah ibn Mawhib, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, he said: If you strike game, meaning if you shoot it in the hill and it dies in the Haram, then expiate; and if you strike in the Haram and it enters the hill and dies, then expiate."
"8151","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah being asked about a man who shoots in the hill, or sends his dog or his bird, while the game is in the Haram, and he said: No."
"8152","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Whatever is not eaten, if you kill it while in ihram, there is no compensation upon you for it, though killing it is forbidden, unless it is hostile or harms you."
"8153","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from A'ishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the killing of five mischievous creatures in the Haram and the hill: the kite, the crow, the mouse, and the vicious dog. He said: As for Ibn Uyaynah, he informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. He said: And Ibn Jurayj mentioned it, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father."
"8154","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Five animals are killed in the hill and the Haram: the crow, the scorpion, the mouse, the kite, and the vicious dog."
"8155","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Whatever beasts attack you, attack it. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar the like of it."
"8156","From al-Aslami, from Zayd ibn Aslam, he said: Abdullah ibn Silan narrated to me that he asked Abu Hurayrah about the vicious dog, and he said: It is the lion."
"8157","From Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, he said: Abdullah ibn Silan narrated to me that he asked Abu Hurayrah about the vicious dog, and he said: It is the lion."
"8158","Al-Thawri informed us, from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-A'la, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab ordered us to kill the snake, the scorpion, the wasp, which is like the bee, which is the dabur, and the mouse, Sufyan doubted, while we were in ihram. From Isra'il, from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-A'la, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, he said: Umar ordered us, the like of it."
"8159","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: Umar was asked about killing the snake. He said: It is an enemy, so kill it wherever you find it, meaning in the Haram and elsewhere."
"8160","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Ammar, he said: I saw Ibn Umar throwing at a crow on his camel while in ihram."
"8161","Muhammad ibn Abi Yahya informed us, from Ibn Harmalah, that he heard Ibn al-Musayyib saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Five the muhrim kills: the scorpion, the snake, the crow, the dog, and the wolf."
"8162","From Hushaym, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Nu'aym, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Five the muhrim kills: the scorpion, the snake, the crow, the dog, and the wolf."
"8163","From Hushaym, from Yazid: The muhrim kills the attacking lion."
"8164","From Hisham, from Ata, he said: The muhrim kills the wolf if it attacks him, and kills such beasts as attack him."
"8165","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Qabisah ibn Dhuwayb, he said: The wolf is killed in the Haram."
"8166","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: Asim ibn Abi al-Najud informed me, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a cave, and there came down upon him: By those sent forth in succession. I took it from his mouth, while his mouth was still moist with it, and I do not know whether it is sealed with: In what narration after it will they believe, or: And when it is said to them, Bow, they do not bow? He said: And a snake slipped into a hole, and he said: You were protected from its evil, and it was protected from your evil. Abd al-Razzaq said: As for Ibn Jurayj, he said: That was at Mina."
"8167","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from his father Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the killing of the gecko, and called it a mischievous one."
"8168","Abbad ibn Kathir informed us, from a man he named, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from A'ishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever kills a gecko, Allah raises for him nine degrees and removes from him nine sins. Al-Qasim said: A'ishah said: Whoever kills a gecko, then turns and prays two rak'ahs, it is for him equal to (freeing) a slave."
"8169","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from A'ishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The frog was extinguishing the fire from Ibrahim, while the gecko was blowing into it, so this one was forbidden to be killed, and that one was ordered to be killed."
"8170","Abu Sa'id al-Shami informed us, from Aban, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Grant safety to the frog, for its voice which you hear is glorification, sanctification, and magnification. The animals asked their Lord to extinguish the fire from Ibrahim, so the frogs were permitted, and they piled themselves upon it, so Allah replaced for them the heat of the fire with water."
"8171","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq, that A'ishah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever kills a gecko, Allah expiates for him seven sins."
"8172","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Jubayr ibn Shaybah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Umm Sharik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered her to kill geckos."
"8173","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Amrah, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Whoever kills a gecko, for him thereby is charity."
"8174","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, narrating it, he said: Whoever kills a gecko, for him is a qirat of reward."
"8175","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, he said: Kill the gecko, for it is a devil."
"8176","Hisham ibn Hassan informed us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The mouse is transformed, with the sign that camel milk is brought near it and it does not taste it, while sheep milk is brought to it and it drinks it. Hammam said to him: Is it something you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? Abu Hurayrah said: Has the Torah then descended upon me?!"
"8177","From al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah ibn Asim, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, he said: A'ishah had a spear with which she killed geckos."
"8178","From Ma'mar, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar used to dislike that the muhrim remove the tick and the qurad from his camel. From Malik, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar the like of it."
"8179","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Harmalah, he said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib was asked about a man who killed a qurad tick or a hutban while in ihram? He said: He gives a date or two dates in charity."
"8180","From Wahb ibn Nafi', and Hisham ibn Hassan, that they heard Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, saying: I was a butcher, and Ibn Abbas said, while I had entered ihram: Rise and delouse this camel. I said: I am in ihram. When he came to al-Suqya, he said: Rise and slaughter this she-camel, so I slaughtered it. Wahb said in his narration: No mother for you, and Hisham said: No mother for the other; woe to you, how many ticks and halamah do you think you have killed."
"8181","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ikrimah, he said: Delousing was mentioned before Ibn Abbas and he disliked it, but when we were on part of the road he ordered me and I slaughtered a she-camel. He said: No mother for you, how many quradah, halamah, and hamnanah do you see in it."
"8182","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, he said: I was a butcher, and Ibn Abbas said, while I had entered ihram: Rise and delouse this camel. I said: I am in ihram. When he came to al-Suqya, he said: Rise and slaughter this she-camel, so I slaughtered it, and he said: No mother for you, how many ticks and halamah do you think you killed in it. Abd al-Razzaq said: And I thought he said: And hamnanah, which is the small qurad."
"8183","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, he said: I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about that which is on the muhrim's camel, when he wants to treat it and cast worms from it, and it was as if he disliked it. Then I asked Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, and he said: Delouse your camel and treat it."
"8184","From Ibn Isa, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Sha'tha, he said: The muhrim may delouse his camel, and smear it with tar."
"8185","From Abdullah ibn Umar, he said: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Rabi'ah ibn Abdullah ibn al-Hudayr narrated to us, he said: I saw Umar ibn al-Khattab delousing his camel at al-Suqya while in ihram, in mud. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Rabi'ah the like of it, except that he did not say: in mud."
"8186","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: A prophet among the prophets camped under a tree, with his luggage beneath it, when an ant bit him, so he ordered his luggage to be lifted, then ordered the tree and it was burned. Allah then revealed to him: Why not one ant, meaning the one which bit him. From Ma'mar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"8187","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever kills a sparrow or smaller without right, its voice is raised to Allah, or he said: it comes to Allah on the Day of Resurrection and says: O Lord, so-and-so killed me without benefit."
"8188","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Suhayb, freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A person who kills a sparrow without its right, Allah will ask him about it. They said: And what is its right? He said: He slaughters it and eats it, and does not cut off its head and throw it away."
"8189","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing four creatures: the ant, the bee, the hoopoe, and the shrike."
"8190","From Abu Sufyan, from al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: If the ant harms you, kill it. He said: And Ibrahim ibn Nafi' informed me, from Ata, the like of Ibrahim's view. He said: And Sulayman al-Ahwal informed me that he heard Tawus saying: We drown it with water. Sufyan said: And Khalid ibn Abi Khalidah informed me, he said: I saw Abu al-Aliyah killing small ants on his mat."
"8191","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umayr, or from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Flies are in the Fire except the bee, and he used to forbid killing them, and forbidding burning food."
"8192","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, he said: I heard Zurarah narrating, from Ibn Abi Nu'm, from Abdullah ibn Umar, he said: Do not kill the frog, for its voice which you hear is glorification and sanctification."
"8193","From al-Aslami, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that he used to forbid the muhrim to kill the kite or lice in the Haram."
"8194","From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Mukhariq ibn Abdullah, he said: I heard Tariq ibn Shihab narrating: That a man called Arbad caught a lizard, and came to Umar, and Umar said to him: Judge in it, so he judged, and Umar approved him."
"8195","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Lahiq ibn Humayd, that he witnessed Ibn Umar and Ibn Safwan, when a man who had struck game came to them and said: Judge upon me. Ibn Umar said to Ibn Safwan: Either you speak and I affirm you, or I speak and you affirm me. Ibn Safwan said: Speak, and I will affirm you. Ibn Umar said: In it is such and such, and Ibn Safwan affirmed him."
"8196","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata about desert waters, are they not sea game? He said: No, and he recited to me: This is sweet fresh, and this is salty bitter. He said: And I asked Ata about the water-fowl, is it land game or sea game, and its likes. He said: Wherever it lives most, that is its game (ruling)."
"8197","From Hushaym, from al-Hajjaj, from Ata, he said: That which lives in the sea and on land, if a muhrim strikes it, upon him is its recompense."
"8198","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that a living soul be confined (as a target)."
"8199","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: He used to dislike the killing of animals, and the killing of monks."
"8200","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the mujaththamah, meaning eating it."
"8201","From al-Thawri, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that a living soul be taken as a target."
"8202","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from al-Minhal, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Umar, that he passed by people who had prepared a hen to shoot at it. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed whoever mutilates animals."
"8203","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that an animal be confined, and forbade eating it taken as a target, toyed with."
"8204","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I heard Tawus, when a man asked him, saying: I scratched while in ihram and killed small ants. He said: Give handfuls in charity."
"8205","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Mutarrif, from Abu Ishaq: That a man killed an Umm Hubayn (bustard), and Uthman judged in it a hamal, which is the young camel foal."
"8206","Al-Thawri informed us, from Ash'ath, from Ata, about the monkey killed in the Haram, and he said: Two just men among you shall judge in it."
"8207","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: There is no compensation in it."
"8208","From a man, from Layth, that he saw Mujahid, while at Arafah an ant bit him in his chest, and he pressed it until he cut off its head in his chest."
"8209","Al-Thawri informed us, and others, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Ziyad, he said: I asked Salim ibn Abdullah about bugs while in ihram, and he said: Kill it, for it is an enemy. Sufyan said: And baqq is the mosquito."
"8210","From al-Thawri, from Ibn al-Abbas al-Amiri, he said: I heard Sa'id ibn Jubayr saying: I would not care, even if I killed so many of them."
"8211","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he: Castrated a camel."
"8212","From Ma'mar, and al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that he: Castrated a mule of his."
"8213","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bashir, from al-Hasan, that he: Saw no harm in it."
"8214","From Malik, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that he: Used to dislike castration, and would say: In it is the growth of creation."
"8215","From al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah ibn Asim, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, that Umar forbade castrating sheep. He said: And is growth except in the males?"
"8216","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas: That no horse be castrated."
"8217","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: One who saw Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz informed me that he castrated horses, then carried upon them in the path of Allah."
"8218","From Abu Ja'far al-Razi, from al-Rabi' ibn Anas, from Anas ibn Malik, about His saying: They will surely change the creation of Allah, he said: Among changing the creation of Allah is castration."
"8219","Wahb ibn Nafi' and al-Muthanna informed us, from al-Qasim ibn Abi Bazzah, he said: Mujahid ordered me to ask Ikrimah about His saying: They will surely change the creation of Allah. He said: It is castration. He said: So I told Mujahid, and he said: He erred; They will surely change the creation of Allah, he said: (means) the religion of Allah. From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: Castration is like it (mutilation)."
"8220","I asked al-Awza'i about castration, and he said: They used to dislike castrating everything that has progeny."
"8221","From Ja'far, he said: Shubayl informed me that he heard Shahr ibn Hawshab saying: Castration is mutilation. He said: And I ordered Ibn al-Nabbah, so he asked al-Hasan about it, and he said: There is no harm in it, meaning castration."
"8222","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a camel branded on its face, and said: Who branded this? They said: al-Abbas. He said: Do you brand on the face, while you are the uncle of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: By Allah, I shall brand only in the farthest thing from the face, and he used to brand on the buttocks."
"8223","Ma'mar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a donkey branded on its face, and said: Allah has cursed whoever did this."
"8224","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a donkey branded on its face, its nostrils smoking, and said: Allah has cursed whoever did this. Let no one brand the face, nor let anyone strike the face."
"8225","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Shu'bah, he said: And Hisham ibn Zayd informed me, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: I entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mirbad while he was branding sheep. Shu'bah said: Most of my thought is that he said: The ear."
"8226","From al-Thawri, from al-Ajlah, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, he said: The people of Kufah wrote with me to Ibn Abbas, and when I came to him the people sufficed me asking him, then a slave man came to him and said: O Abu Abbas, I shoot game, killing outright and wounding. He said: What you kill outright, eat; and what disappears from you overnight, do not eat, for I do not know whether you killed it or another. He said: I am a slave man; a passer passes by me and asks me for milk to drink, so I give him to drink. He said: If you fear he will die of thirst, give him to drink what sustains him till another, then seek permission of your family for what you gave. He said: Then I find the sea has cast forth fish. He said: Then do not eat of it any floating one."
"8227","From Isra'il, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: He was asked about the man who shoots game and finds his arrow in it the next day. He said: If I knew your arrow killed it, I would order you to eat it, but I do not know; perhaps cold killed it, or other than that."
"8228","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, he said: A man came to him and said: I shoot game, killing outright and wounding. He said: What you kill outright, eat; and what you wound, do not eat."
"8229","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ziyad ibn Abi Maryam, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I shot game and it disappeared from me overnight. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The vermin of the night are many. And Abd al-Razzaq holds by this."
"8230","From Ma'mar, from a man, from Ikrimah, saying: If you find an arrow in game that has died, do not eat it, for you do not know who shot it, nor whether he named (Allah) or not."
"8231","From Ma'mar, from Asim, from al-Sha'bi, from Adi ibn Hatim, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I shoot game and it disappears from me overnight. He said: If you find in it your arrow, and find in it no other trace, then eat it."
"8232","Ma'mar informed us, from al-A'mash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: I asked Abu al-Darda about game I shot and it disappeared from me overnight, then I found in it my arrow, finding nothing else in it. He said: As for me, I would have eaten it."
"8233","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I shot game and it fell, and I kept looking at it until it died. He said: Eat it. He said: But if it is hidden from you by mountains or hills, and its place of falling is hidden from you, then leave it."
"8234","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq, from Qays ibn Aslam, from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali, from A'ishah: That a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a gazelle he had struck yesterday, dead. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I recognized in it my arrow, and I had shot it yesterday. He said: If I knew your arrow killed it, I would have eaten it, but I do not know; the vermin of the night are many; if I knew your arrow killed it, I would have eaten it."
"8235","From Ma'mar, and al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abdullah ibn Murrah, from Masruq, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: If one of you shoots a bird while it is on a mountain and it dies, do not eat it, for I fear its fall killed it, or it fell into water and died, so do not eat it, for I fear the water killed it."
"8236","Ma'mar informed us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: If you shoot game and it falls, or falls into water and dies, do not eat it."
"8237","Ma'mar informed us, from Isma'il ibn Sharus, from Ikrimah, he said: If you shoot a bird and it falls into water before you slaughter it, do not eat it."
"8238","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Isa ibn Abi Azzah, from Amir al-Sha'bi, that he was brought bird meat which a man had shot and slaughtered, then left it, and it flew and fell into water and died, so he refused to eat of it, and said: It helped against itself."
"8239","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: If you shoot game and it falls into water, then if it is water game, there is no harm in eating it."
"8240","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: I shot game and hit its vital spot, then it fell or fell into water, while I was looking at it, and died. He said: Do not eat it."
"8241","Ma'mar informed us, from one who heard Ikrimah saying: If you strike game and a limb falls from it, then it runs on alive, do not eat that limb, but eat the rest of it in which is the head; but if it dies when you struck it, eat all of it, what fell from it and what did not fall. Abd al-Razzaq said: And Uthman ibn Matar said it, from Sa'id, from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim."
"8242","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: If you strike it and a limb falls from it, then it runs on, do not eat the one that fell, but eat the rest of it."
"8243","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you shoot a bird with a stone and cut off a limb from it, and catch it alive, then the limb from it is dead meat, but slaughter what remains of it and eat it. And if you stab game with your spear and kill it, or strike it with your sword and cut it off, and it was it (the killing blow), then eat it."
"8244","Al-Thawri informed us, he said: If he cuts the two thighs and severs them, he does not eat the two thighs, but eats what has the head. If with the two thighs is what is less than half the beast, he does not eat it, but eats what is toward the head. If the two halves are equal, he eats them both together, and eat whatever exceeds from the head side. And it is the view of Abu Hanifah."
"8245","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about Haram game when found in the hill. He said: If you find it in the hill, hunt it and eat it."
"8246","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ziyad ibn Abi Maryam, he said: A man had a wild donkey which escaped in his house and they could not catch it, so he struck its neck with the sword and named (Allah), then asked Ibn Mas'ud about that, and he ordered him to eat it."
"8247","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim, from Ziyad ibn Abi Maryam: That a donkey of the family of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, of the wild, they struggled with it and it overcame them, and attacked them, so they killed it. Ibn Mas'ud said: Hasten the slaughter, and saw no harm in it."
"8248","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, he said: Ash'ath ibn Abi al-Sha'tha informed me, from his father, and al-Harith ibn Suwayd, they said: We came to the house of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, and his boys had seized a wild donkey, and one of them struck it with his sword on its nostril. He said: Do you think Abdullah will eat of it? He said: So we sat with him to see what he would do. He said: Then we were brought a bowl of it. He said: So we mentioned to him what we had seen, and he said: It is only game."
"8249","From Isra'il, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: If the camel bolts, shoot it with your arrow, mention the name of Allah, and eat."
"8250","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, he said: A man came to Ali and said: A camel of mine bolted and I stabbed it with the spear. Ali said: Gift me its rump."
"8251","Al-Thawri informed us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Whatever of the animals defeats you is in the position of game."
"8252","From Ja'far, from Awf, he said: A man struck a camel's neck with the sword and severed it, and asked Ali ibn Abi Talib about it. He said: A slaughter, and alive (valid)."
"8253","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: He was asked about a man who had a gazelle with him, and feared it would escape, so he shot it with an arrow. He said: He eats it."
"8254","From al-Thawri, from his father, from Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Dhu al-Hulayfah of Tihamah, and the people got camels and sheep, and hastened with them, and boiled them in the pots. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to them and ordered the pots, so they were overturned. He equated ten sheep with a camel. He said: And a camel among them bolted, and a man shot it with an arrow and restrained it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: These camels have wildness like the wildness of wild beasts, so whatever overcomes you of them, do with it thus. He said: Then Rafi' ibn Khadij came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, we fear we shall meet the enemy, or it is hoped we shall meet the enemy tomorrow, and we have no knives, so shall we slaughter with reeds? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever spills the blood, and the name of Allah is mentioned, then eat; not the tooth and the nail, and I shall tell: as for the tooth, it is bone, and as for the nail, it is the knives of the Abyssinians. Rifa'ah said: Then a water-carrying (camel) fell into a well in Madinah and was slaughtered from its side, meaning its flank, and Umar took from it a tenth (?) for a dirham."
"8255","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he said: About the animal that turns wild, he said: It is in the position of game, or: it is game."
"8256","Ma'mar informed us, from a man, from Ikrimah, he disliked eating the slaughter of play, saying: If you stab it or slaughter it with the sword playfully, do not eat it."
"8257","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: If you shoot a ram or a rooster with arrows and kill it, do not eat it, for it is dead meat, and everything of play, do not eat it."
"8258","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If a stallion attacks a man and kills him, he said: They say: He compensates for him. Ata said: And its meat is not eaten."
"8259","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: There is no slaughter except in the throat and the slaughter-place."
"8260","Ibn Jurayj said: And Dawud ibn Abi Asim informed me, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: None is sacrificed except in the sacrifice-place of Ibrahim, meaning: none is slaughtered in its flank, nor elsewhere."
"8261","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, he said: I heard Ibn al-Musayyib saying: Wherever your weapon falls on game, eat; but the domestic, no, until it is slaughtered or sacrificed."
"8262","From Isra'il, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: If the camel falls into the well, stab it from its flank, mention the name of Allah, and eat."
"8263","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, and al-A'mash, from Abu al-Duha: That a sheep fell into a well, and Masruq said: Slaughter it from its flank."
"8264","From Ma'mar, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Slaughter it from wherever you are able to do that."
"8265","Ma'mar informed us, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, about the Muslim who borrows a Magian's dog and sends it upon game, he said: His dog is like his knife, saying: There is no harm in it. Qatadah said: And al-Hasan disliked it."
"8266","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: There is no harm in that when the Muslim is the one who releases and mentions the name."
"8267","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: If you release the trained dog of a Magian and it kills, then eat."
"8268","Ma'mar and Muhammad ibn Muslim reported to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: Nothing of the Magian's hunt is eaten except fish and locusts."
"8269","From Amr ibn Ruwayman, from al-Hajjaj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: Do not eat the hunt of the Magian's dog, nor what his arrow strikes. And Ata said the same, and there is no harm in his bread."
"8270","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: There is no harm in the bread of the Magian."
"8271","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, regarding His saying: and what you have taught of hunting creatures, training them - of dogs and other trained creatures, of falcons, hawks, cheetahs and the like; he said: and I know it only as mentioned from Ibn Abbas."
"8272","Ma'mar reported to us, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: He was asked about the falcon, the hawk, the cheetah and what one hunts with of predators, and he said: All of these are jawarih. Ma'mar said: And Hammad said that, except that the falcon and hawk, if they eat of their game it is eaten, but if the dog and the cheetah eat it is not eaten. Abd al-Razzaq said: And I heard it from Ma'mar more than once, the hadith of Layth."
"8273","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: And I heard him, meaning Ata, say: If you release a trained dog upon game, and an untrained dog comes upon the game and both join in killing it, then do not eat."
"8274","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Ubayd ibn Umayr say: If you release your trained dog and your trained hawk, then eat, even if they kill."
"8275","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: The case of the dog and the hawk is one."
"8276","From Ma'mar, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from al-Sha'bi, from Adi ibn Hatim, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah! My land is a land of hunting. He said: When you release your trained dog and mention the name, eat of what your dog holds for you, even if it kills; but if it eats, do not eat of it, for it only held it for itself. And if you release your dog and dogs over which the name of Allah was not mentioned mix with it, do not eat, for you do not know which of them killed it."
"8277","He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah! I shoot game and it disappears from me for a night. He said: If you find your arrow in it and find nothing else in it, eat it."
"8278","Ma'mar reported to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Abu Tha'laba al-Khushani, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah! Write for me such-and-such land, which had not yet been conquered at that time. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you not hear what this man says? Abu Tha'laba said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, you will surely conquer it, O Messenger of Allah! So he wrote it for him. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, our land is a land of hunting; I release my trained dog and my untrained dog. He said: When you release your trained dog and mention the name, eat of what your dog holds for you, even if it kills; and when you release your untrained dog and you reach its slaughter in time, eat; and eat of what your arrow returns to you, even if it kills. He said: I said: And mention the name of Allah."
"8279","He said: I said: O Prophet of Allah! Our land is the land of the People of the Book, and they eat pork and drink wine; what shall we do with their vessels and pots? He said: If you find nothing else, wash them with water, and cook in them and drink."
"8280","He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah! What is lawful for us of what is forbidden to us? He said: Do not eat the flesh of domestic donkeys, nor every predator with fangs."
"8281","From Ma'mar, from Amr, he said: Al-Hasan was asked about a man who finds game with his dog and finds nothing with which to slaughter it, so he leaves it in its grip until it kills it. He said: There is no harm in eating it."
"8282","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: If your dog seizes game and you take it from it while alive, and it dies in your hand before you slaughter it, do not eat it."
"8283","From Ma'mar, he said: I heard a man asking Qatada about a man who was training a falcon of his; while it was circling around him it saw a bird and swooped upon it, and the man mentioned the name. He said: Do not eat it unless you reach its slaughter in time, because he did not release it himself."
"8284","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, he said: They used to dislike releasing the hunting dog upon carrion."
"8285","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: The hunt of the jet-black dog was disliked, because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded killing it."
"8286","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, regarding a man who shot an arrow and killed, and forgot to mention the name: he said: He eats it."
"8287","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, regarding a man who went out intending to hunt, put on his bow and his gear and mentioned the name, then saw game suddenly and shot it and forgot to mention the name: he said: There is no harm in eating it. Ma'mar said it, and al-Zuhri and Qatada said it."
"8288","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, regarding two dogs that seized game and divided it between them: if they have not eaten of it, he eats."
"8289","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, that Ibn Mas'ud said regarding the trained dog that eats: he said: Do not eat of it, for had it been trained it would not eat of it."
"8290","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: If the trained dog eats, do not eat of it, for it only held for itself."
"8291","From Abu Hanifa, from Hammad, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: If the trained dog eats, do not eat; but as for the falcon and the hawk, if they eat, it is eaten."
"8292","Al-Thawri reported to us, from Ash'ath, from al-Sha'bi, he said: If the dog drinks of the blood of the game, do not eat it."
"8293","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: Eat whatever your trained dog eats of, even if it eats. From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar likewise."
"8294","From Sa'id ibn Abi Aruba, from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Salman, he said regarding the trained dog: he eats of what it holds. He said: Eat even if it eats two-thirds of it. He said: And Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas said: Eat even if nothing remains but its head."
"8295","Ibn Jurayj reported to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: Of birds, the falcon and others hunt; if you reach its slaughter in time, eat, otherwise do not taste it. As for the trained dog, eat of what it holds for you, even if it eats of it. From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar likewise."
"8296","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: If the dog eats of the game, do not eat it."
"8297","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmala, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Every wild animal you kill with a stone, a pellet, a stick or the like, eat it; and if you shoot and forget to mention the name, mention and eat."
"8298","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Harmala, he said: I heard Ibn al-Musayyib say: Every wild animal you kill with a stone or a pellet, eat; but if you refuse to eat, bring it to me. The man said: I hastened and forgot to mention the name of Allah. He said: Mention and eat."
"8299","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Ammar ibn Yasir, he said: If you shoot with a stone or a pellet, then mention the name of Allah, eat."
"8300","Ibn Uyayna said: And a brother of Ibn Abi Layla informed me, he said: I shot a bird, or he said: game, with a pellet and killed it, and I asked Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, and he ordered me to eat it."
"8301","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', he said: I shot game with a stone, and Ibn Umar took it and said: O Abu Nafi'. He said: O my son, bring me something with which to slaughter it. He said: I hastened and brought him the adze, and he began slaughtering it with the edge of the adze, and it died in his hand, so he threw it away."
"8302","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan and Mujahid: They disliked the hunt of the pellet-bow unless its slaughter is reached in time."
"8303","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: If you shoot game with a pellet and reach its slaughter in time, eat it, otherwise do not eat it."
"8304","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, that he disliked game being killed with the pellet then eaten."
"8305","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: If you shoot game and it dies in your hand, eat it; but if you take it and wish to keep it, and it dies in your hand, do not eat it."
"8306","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Adi ibn Hatim, he said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the hunt of the mi'rad. He said: If it pierces, eat."
"8307","From Ibn Uyayna, he said: Mujalid narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from Adi ibn Hatim, he said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the hunt of the mi'rad. He said: Do not eat of it except what you slaughter."
"8308","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said regarding the hunt of the mi'rad: If it pierces, there is no harm in it; and if you shoot with an arrow with no iron head and it falls, eat it."
"8309","From Ma'mar, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, he said: The people of Medina went out to a gathering of theirs, and I saw a bald left-handed man who towered above the people by a forearm, wearing a coarse waist-wrapper and a coarse cotton cloak, wrapped in it, saying: O people! Emigrate and do not turn back, and let none of you strike the rabbit with a stick or a stone then eat it; but let the asal slaughter for you: spears and arrows. I said: Who is this? They said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"8310","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Zirr, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab say: O people! Emigrate and do not turn back, and let each of you beware of striking the rabbit with a stick or shooting it with a stone; but let the asal slaughter for you: spears and arrows."
"8311","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysara, from Ubayd ibn Sa'd, he said: Bilal shot a rabbit with a stick, broke its legs, then slaughtered it and ate it."
"8312","From al-Aslami, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, he said: I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about the hunt of the pellet and the mi'rad. He said: Salman was asked about it and said: If you do not eat it, bring it to me and I shall eat it."
"8313","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, regarding the mi'rad: If it strikes broadside, eat it, otherwise it is dead meat; and if you shoot with an arrow with no iron head, it is carrion; and if you shoot with an arrow with an iron head, it is likewise."
"8314","Ma'mar reported to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Muslim is a name among the names of Allah; if one of you forgets to mention the name over the slaughter, let him mention and eat."
"8315","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: With the Muslim is the remembrance of Allah; if he slaughters and forgets to mention the name, let him mention and eat. And if the Magian mentions the name of Allah over his slaughter, it is not eaten."
"8316","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, regarding a man who slaughters and forgets to mention the name: he said: No harm."
"8317","From al-Thawri, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, that he was asked about a man who slaughters and forgets to mention the name: he said: No harm; mention over it and eat it."
"8318","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, he said: People who had embraced Islam in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Medina with meat they were selling; the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were averse to it and said: Perhaps the name of Allah was not mentioned over it. So they asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Mention you yourselves and eat."
"8319","Ma'mar reported to us, from one who heard Ikrima say: The slaughter of boastful poets slaughtered boastfully is not eaten, nor the slaughter for gambling. He said: And Ikrima was asked: May the junub slaughter? He said: Yes, and let him perform ablution."
"8320","From his father, he said: Mina informed me, he said: Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf had a domestic sheep; it urinated on his bed, so he rose to it angrily and slaughtered it while angry and did not mention the name. He said: I came to Abu Hurayra and mentioned that to him, and he said: No harm; let him mention over it when he eats."
"8321","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Abi Layla, and Isma'il ibn Muslim, from al-Hakam, he said: I asked Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla about the slaughter of a Muslim who forgets to mention the name of Allah. He said: It is eaten; slaughter is upon the religion. Do you not see that if a Magian mentions the name of Allah over his slaughter, it is not eaten. And Ibn Jurayj said, from Ata: He distinguished that by the Book."
"8322","Yahya ibn Muslim reported to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Ata, he said: If the Muslim says: In the name of Satan, eat."
"8323","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Sha'tha, he said: Ayn, meaning Ikrima, narrated to us, from Ibn Abbas, he said: In the Muslim is the name of Allah; if he slaughters and forgets the name of Allah, let him eat; but if the Magian slaughters and mentions the name of Allah, do not eat it."
"8324","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', that a slave-girl of Ka'b ibn Malik was grazing sheep of hers, and a sheep was dying, so she slaughtered it with a marwa, and he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he ordered eating it. Abd al-Razzaq said: Al-Marwa is stone. Abdullah ibn Umar reported to us, from Nafi', from Sulayman ibn Yasar: that a slave-girl of Ka'b, and he mentioned the like, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Sulayman ibn Yasar."
"8325","From Isra'il, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Whoever slaughters, young or old, male or female, eat. From Ma'mar, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi likewise."
"8326","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: There is no harm in the slaughter of a boy and a woman, of Muslims and the People of the Book."
"8327","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, he said: My father was asked about the slaughter of a boy. He said: When he holds the knife."
"8328","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: He used to see no harm in the slaughter of a boy when he understands slaughter and mentions the name."
"8329","Qays ibn al-Rabi' reported to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ali al-Azdi, he said: I asked Ibn Umar: We travel to foreign lands, and a Bedouin or a boy meets us and feeds us meat, we do not know what it is. He said: Eat whatever the Muslim feeds you."
"8330","From al-Aslami, from Dawud ibn Abi Salih, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, that Umar ibn al-Khattab came to the butchers and said: Who slaughters for you? They said: This foreigner. Umar questioned him and he did not do it well, so Umar flogged him some lashes, then said: Let none slaughter for you except one who understands prayer."
"8331","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: that people were in the market, newly Muslim with no understanding, not slaughtering well. Umar ibn al-Khattab expelled them from the market and ordered their expulsion."
"8332","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Nafi', he said: I heard a man of Banu Salima narrate to Abdullah ibn Umar that a slave-girl of Ka'b ibn Malik was grazing sheep of his at Sila'; death came to a sheep among them, so she took a sharp stone, broke it and slaughtered with it, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to eat it."
"8333","Ma'mar reported to us, from a man, from Ikrima, that he used to dislike eating the slaughter of Bedouins slaughtered at their graves. Abd al-Razzaq said: Zurara is a stone broken at an edge."
"8334","Ma'mar reported to us, from Qatada, he said: Ibn Abbas used to dislike the slaughter of the uncircumcised and say: His testimony is not valid, nor is his prayer accepted. Ma'mar said: I asked Hammad about it, and he said: There is no harm in his slaughter; his testimony is valid and his prayer accepted."
"8335","Ma'mar said: And al-Hasan used to permit a man who embraced Islam after growing old and feared hardship upon himself if circumcised, not to be circumcised; and he saw no harm in eating his slaughter."
"8336","From Ma'mar, he said: I said to Qatada: An uncircumcised man milks a sheep or a cow. He said: No harm in it. Qatada said: And if a woman who has not menstruated slaughters, there is no harm in her slaughter."
"8337","From Qays ibn al-Rabi', from Isma'il ibn Sami', from Malik ibn Umayr, from Walan Abu Urwa al-Muradi, he said: I returned to my family and found a sheep of ours slaughtered. I said to my family: What is the matter with it? They said: We feared it would die. He said: And in the house was a captive boy of ours who had not prayed, and he slaughtered it. So I came to Ibn Mas'ud and asked him, and he said: Eat it."
"8338","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he used not to eat the slaughter of the Zanj. He said: I said to Ibn Tawus: Why? He said: My father used to say: Have you ever seen any good in a Zanj?"
"8339","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, he said: I asked al-Sha'bi about the slaughter of the mute. He said: He points to the sky."
"8340","From Ma'mar, from Amr ibn Muslim, he said: I asked Tawus and Ikrima about the slaughter of the thief, and they disliked it and forbade me eating it."
"8341","From one of his companions, from al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Hudhali, he said: I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about a slave who steals a sheep or a cow and slaughters it; he saw no harm in his slaughter."
"8342","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about the slaughter of the thief. He said: No harm in it."
"8343","Ma'mar reported to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abida al-Salmani, that Ali used to dislike the slaughter of the Christians of Banu Taghlib, saying: They hold of Christianity nothing but drinking wine."
"8344","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about the slaughters of the Arab Christians. He said: Whoever adopts a religion is of its people, and he saw no harm in their slaughters."
"8345","From Ata al-Khurasani, he said: No harm in their slaughters. Have you not heard Allah say: And among them are unlettered ones who do not know the Book."
"8346","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, he said: And whoever takes them as allies among you, then he is of them."
"8347","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: No harm in their slaughters."
"8348","From al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Allah only permitted their slaughters, and your Lord is never forgetful."
"8349","Al-Thawri reported to us, from Abu al-Ala Burd ibn Sinan, from Ubada ibn Nasiy, from Ghutayf ibn al-Harith, he said: An official wrote to Umar: That before us are people called the Samaritans; they read the Torah, observe the Sabbath, and do not believe in resurrection; what does the Commander of the Faithful see regarding their slaughters? Umar wrote to him: They are a sect of the People of the Book; their slaughters are the slaughters of the People of the Book."
"8350","From Ma'mar, from a man, from Ikrima, he said: No harm in the slaughters of the People of the Book, but he disliked a Muslim handing his sheep to a Jew to slaughter it."
"8351","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Qays ibn Sakan, he said: Ibn Mas'ud said: You have settled a land where Muslims do not slaughter; they are but Nabateans and Persians. If you buy meat, ask; if it is the slaughter of a Jew or a Christian, eat it, for their food is lawful to you."
"8352","From al-Thawri, from one of his companions, from al-Hakam, from Abu Iyad, that he permitted their slaughters but disliked their women."
"8353","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, that he was asked about a Jew who slaughtered a sheep and erred in it so that it became forbidden to him. He said: It is not lawful for a Muslim to eat it. If a man of the People of the Book offers you food, order him to eat; if he eats, eat, and if he does not eat, do not eat it."
"8354","Ma'mar reported to us, from Amr ibn Maymun ibn Mihran, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz entrusted Muslims over Christians when they slaughter, to mention the name, and not to leave them invoking other than Allah."
"8355","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, and Qatada said: Camels and cattle, if you wish slaughter by throat-cutting, and if you wish by nahr."
"8356","Al-Thawri reported to us, from Ubayd, from Mujahid, he said: Both slaughter and nahr were among them, regarding His saying: then they slaughtered it though they almost did not, and His saying: so pray to your Lord and sacrifice."
"8357","From al-Rabi', from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Allah mentioned slaughtering the cow in the Quran; if you slaughter what is nahr'd, it suffices you."
"8358","Ibn Jurayj said: And Ata said: Slaughter is cutting the jugular veins. I said: He slaughtered but did not cut its jugulars until it died, thinking he had cut its jugulars? He said: I see it only as slaughtered; let him eat."
"8359","Ma'mar reported to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar used to dislike eating a slaughter slaughtered facing other than the qibla."
"8360","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: I asked him about a man who slaughters toward the qibla then turns away from the qibla. He said: No harm in it. He said: And Jabir said: He said: It does not harm you whether you face the qibla or not."
"8361","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, and al-Thawri, from Ash'ath, from Ibn Sirin, he said: It was deemed good that the slaughter be faced toward the qibla."
"8362","From Isma'il ibn Abdullah, from Abdullah ibn Awn, he said: I asked al-Qasim about a man who slaughters facing other than the qibla, is his slaughter eaten? He said: What harm in that?"
"8363","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', he said: Ibn Umar would not eat the sheep when nakh' was done."
"8364","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said regarding the sheep when nakh' is done: It is disliked, but no harm in eating it."
"8365","Ma'mar reported to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar would not eat the sheep when nakh' was done."
"8366","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Safar, and from Abu Ishaq, from al-Sha'bi, that he was asked about a rooster slaughtered from the back of its neck. He said: If you wish, eat."
"8367","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, that he was asked about the slaughter when slaughtered and the knife passes and cuts the whole neck. He said: No harm in it."
"8368","From Ma'mar, from Jabir, he said: I asked al-Sha'bi about a man who slaughters birds from the back of the neck. He saw no harm in it."
"8369","From Mughira, from Ibrahim, that he was asked about the slaughter when slaughtered and the knife passes and cuts the whole neck. He said: A quick slaughter; he said: No harm in eating it."
"8370","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, that Ali said: The chicken when its head is cut off: a quick slaughter; I eat it."
"8371","From al-Hasan ibn Umara, from al-Hakam ibn Utayba, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, he said: He was asked about the chicken slaughtered when the knife slips and cuts the head. He said: If he did not intend it, let him eat it."
"8372","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Abi Najih, he said: Whoever slaughters a camel from behind deliberately, it is not eaten; and if he slaughters a sheep from its joint deliberately, meaning the joint deliberately, it is not eaten."
"8373","Ibn Jurayj reported to us, from Ata, he said: If a slaughterer slaughters and severs the head, eat so long as he did not intend that."
"8374","From Ma'mar, he said: Al-Zuhri was asked about a man who slaughters with his sword and cuts the head. He said: Evil what he did. The man said: May he eat it? He said: Yes."
"8375","Ma'mar reported to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: If a man slaughters a kid and cuts its head, there is no harm in eating it."
"8376","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, that he was asked about the slaughter when slaughtered and the knife passes and cuts the whole neck. He said: No harm in it."
"8377","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Abu al-Ash'ath al-San'ani, from Shaddad ibn Aws, he said: I memorized two things from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Allah is Muhsin, loves ihsan to everything; when you kill, do excellently in killing, and when you slaughter, do excellently in slaughter; let each of you sharpen his blade and comfort his slaughter."
"8378","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilaba, from Abu al-Ash'ath, from Shaddad, he said: I memorized two things from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he said: Allah has prescribed ihsan upon everything; when you kill, do excellently in killing, and when you slaughter, do excellently in slaughter; let each of you sharpen his blade and comfort his slaughter."
"8379","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab saw a man dragging a sheep by its leg to slaughter it, and said to him: Woe to you! Lead it to death with a kindly leading."
"8380","From al-Thawri, from a man, from Salih, freedman of al-Taw'ama, from Abu Hurayra, he said: I heard him say: When one of you sharpens the blade, let him not sharpen it while the sheep looks at him. From al-Aslami, that he heard Salih, freedman of al-Taw'ama, narrate it from Abu Hurayra."
"8381","From Ma'mar, from Asim, from Ikrima, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man lay a sheep down, put his foot on its neck while sharpening his blade. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Woe to you! Did you intend to kill it many deaths? Why did you not sharpen your blade before laying it down?"
"8382","Muhammad ibn Rashid reported to us, he said: Al-Wadin ibn Ata narrated to me: that a butcher opened a door upon a sheep to slaughter it; it escaped from him until it came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he followed it; he seized it dragging it by its leg. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to it: Be patient with Allah's decree; and you, O butcher, drive it to death with a gentle driving."
"8383","From al-Aslami, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab used to forbid slaughtering a sheep in front of a sheep."
"8384","From Ma'mar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, he said: The people of Jahiliyya used to cut humps and sever fat tails; they asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, and he said: Whatever is cut from the beast while alive is dead meat."
"8385","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, he said: The people of Jahiliyya used to cut the fat tails of sheep and the humps of camels. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever is cut from the beast while alive is dead meat."
"8386","From Ibn Uyayna, from Rukayn ibn Rabi', from Abu Talha, he said: A wolf attacked a sheep and tore its belly, and something fell from it to the ground; I asked Ibn Abbas, and he said: Look at what fell to the ground, do not eat it; and he ordered him to slaughter it and eat it."
"8387","Ma'mar reported to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from a man, from Ibn al-Farafisa al-Hanafi, from his father, that he said to Umar: You slaughter slaughters that are not lawful; you hasten upon the slaughter. Umar said: We are more entitled to guard against that, O Abu Hayyan! Slaughter is in the throat and the labba for one who can; and leave souls until they depart."
"8388","Ma'mar reported to us, and al-Thawri, from Ayyub, from Abdullah ibn Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Slaughter is in the throat and the labba."
"8389","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Slaughter is cutting the jugular veins. I said: A slaughterer slaughters but does not cut its jugulars. He said: I see it only as slaughtered; let him eat it."
"8390","From Ma'mar, from Awf al-Abdi, from Abu Raja al-Utaridi, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about rabbits I slaughtered with my nail. He said: Do not eat them, for they are strangled."
"8391","From al-Thawri, from his father, from Abaya ibn Rifa'a, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever causes blood to flow and the name of Allah is mentioned over it, eat, not tooth and nail. And I shall tell you: as for the tooth, it is bone; and as for the nail, it is the knife of the Abyssinians."
"8392","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: Slaughter with everything except four: tooth, nail, horn and bone."
"8393","Hisham ibn Hassan reported to us, from al-Hasan, he said: Everything that severs the jugulars and sheds blood, except nail, fang and bone."
"8394","From Isra'il, from Simak ibn Harb, from Murri ibn Qatari, from Adi ibn Hatim, he said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about game I hunt. He said: Cause blood to flow with whatever you wish, and mention the name of Allah over it."
"8395","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, regarding a man who slaughters with a stick: If it cuts, without bruising or breaking, then no harm in it."
"8396","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: If you have no blade, then slaughter a sheep with a peg, it suffices you."
"8397","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Slaughter with a stick when it severs the jugulars, without bruising."
"8398","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, that Safina, freedman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), shed the blood of a camel with a trunk, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to eat it."
"8399","From Ibn Uyayna, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, that a boy of the Ansar of Banu Haritha was grazing a milch-camel at Uhud; death came to it, and he had no iron with which to slaughter it, so he took a peg of sticks and nahr'd it with it, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to eat it."
"8400","From Ibn Uyayna, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, that a boy of the Ansar was grazing a camel of his at Uhud; he feared death for it, so he nahr'd it with a wooden peg, asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he ordered him to eat it."
"8401","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abu Hazim, he said: I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about a camel slaughtered with a stick. He said: If it penetrated flowing, eat; and if it did not penetrate, do not eat it."
"8402","From Ibn Uyayna, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, he said: I heard Ibn al-Musayyib say: Everything that cuts, slaughter therein when forced to it."
"8403","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Sha'tha, he said: If it severs the jugulars, eat."
"8404","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Matar ibn Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Ibn al-Khattab, that he said: No slaughter except in the asal."
"8405","Ma'mar reported to us, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: I asked him about a man who lays his sickle, and a bird passes by it and splits its belly and kills it; he disliked eating it. He said: And I asked Salim ibn Abdullah about it, and he saw no harm in it."
"8406","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: If you slaughter it and it twitches its tail or moves, that suffices you."
"8407","From al-Aslami, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, and Ibn Jurayj mentioned it, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, that Ali said: If it strikes with its tail or leg, or blinks its eye, it is valid slaughter."
"8408","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said to me: The strangled, the fallen, the gored, and what the beast of prey eats of; he said: If you slaughter it while its eye blinks, or a leg of its legs, there is no harm in it."
"8409","Ma'mar reported to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hibban, from Abu Murra, freedman of Aqil, that he found a sheep of theirs dying, so he slaughtered it and it moved. He said: I asked Zayd ibn Thabit, and he said: The carcass moves. He said: And he asked Abu Hurayra, and he said: Eat it if its eye blinks, or a leg of its legs moves. From Ibn Uyayna, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hibban, from Abu Murra, freedman of Aqil, likewise."
"8410","From Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Ubayd ibn Umayr say: If it blinks, twitches its tail, or moves, it has become lawful."
"8411","From Ibn Jurayj: a man asked Ata, saying: a sheep fell down and its head was cut off, while it was moving and not dead — should it be slaughtered? He said: No. He said: I asked him again, and he said: keep away from it."
"8412","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: he said about the fetus: when it has grown hair or wool, its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother."
"8413","From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Zuhri, from Abd Allah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik: he said: the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: when the fetus grows hair, its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother."
"8414","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: he said about the fetus: if it comes out dead, having grown hair or wool, its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother. Ma'mar said: al-Hasan and Qatadah said the same."
"8415","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: he said: when it grows hair or wool, its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother. Ma'mar said: and one who heard Ikrimah informed me that he says the same."
"8416","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, about the fetus: if its mother casts it dead after she has been sacrificed, then eat it, because she cast it after she was sacrificed."
"8417","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: he said: its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother, whether it has grown hair or not, unless it is foul."
"8418","From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Hasan ibn Ubayd Allah al-Nakha'i: he said: I asked Ibrahim about the fetus of a cow, and he said: it is only a part of its parts."
"8419","Ibn Jurayj reported: he said: I heard Dawud ibn Abi Asim say: I stayed in a house in Madinah, and I sacrificed a she-camel in it, and she cast a young camel from her belly, dead — meaning the fetus that had not grown hair — so I asked Ibn al-Musayyib, and he said: eat it. He said: so I returned and took it, and I lived off its liver and hump as I wished."
"8420","Ma'mar reported, from one who heard Ikrimah say: if the fetus comes out alive, then dies before you slaughter it, do not eat it. And Ibn Jurayj said it from Ata."
"8421","From Ibn al-Mubarak, from Ibn Abi Layla, from his brother, or from al-Hakam — Ibn al-Mubarak doubted — from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla: he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: the slaughter of the fetus is the slaughter of its mother, whether it has grown hair or not."
"8422","From Ibn al-Mubarak, from Mujalid ibn Sa'id, from Abu al-Waddak, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri: he said: we asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the fetus, and he said: eat it if you wish."
"8423","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, about His saying, Exalted: game of the sea and its food, as provision for you: he said: its game is what you hunt from it, and its food is what you carry salted in your travel."
"8424","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah: that Ibn Umar said: its food is what it throws up, and its game is what you hunt."
"8425","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Abu Mijlaz: that Abu Bakr said: fish are pure, alive and dead. Qatadah said: and what floats upon the water, there is no harm in it."
"8426","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bashir, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas: he said: I testify regarding Abu Bakr: he said: the floating fish is lawful; whoever wants it may eat it."
"8427","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Zubayr, from the freedman of Abu Bakr: he said: every creature in the sea, Allah has slaughtered it, so eat it."
"8428","Ma'mar reported, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir: he said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the sea? And he said: its dead is lawful, its water is purifying."
"8429","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir: he said: al-Mughirah ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd reported that some people of Banu Mudlij asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Messenger of Allah! We ride upon rafts of ours, and one of us carries a little water for his drinking; if we perform ablution with sea water we feel distress in ourselves, and if we perform ablution from it we thirst. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: its water is purifying, its dead is lawful."
"8430","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar: he said: I heard an elder who had met the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: everything of sea game is slaughtered."
"8431","Al-Thawri reported, from al-Ajlah, from Abd Allah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl: he said: I heard Ibn Abbas say: do not eat what floats."
"8432","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: he said: if you find it floating, do not eat it, for its being taken is its slaughter — meaning fish in the sea."
"8433","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah: he said: Abu Bakr said: the food of the sea — eat all that is in it. Amr said: I mentioned it to Abu al-Sha'tha, and he said: we used to narrate only that its food is salted, and we dislike the floating thereof; as for what the water has receded from, then eat it."
"8434","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir: he said: what you find floating, do not eat it, and what is on its banks, eat it. Sufyan said: it is left by the receding water only from a living one."
"8435","Al-Thawri reported, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Ali: he said: fish and locusts are all pure."
"8436","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zinad, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from Thuwayb: he said: the sea cast up much dead fish, so we asked Abu Hurayrah for a ruling, and he ordered eating it; then we turned away from Abu Hurayrah's ruling, so we told Marwan, and he sent to Zayd ibn Thabit asking him, and he said: lawful, so eat it. From Ma'mar, from Abu al-Zinad, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Thuwayb: that the sea — and he mentioned similar."
"8437","Ma'mar reported, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Wahb ibn Kaysan: he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent us in a detachment, and provisioned us a bag of dates; when we went out we spent what was with us, and our provisions ran out, and nothing remained with us except the bag. Each one of us was given a date. He said: I said: did it benefit you? Jabir said: indeed we felt its loss. He said: we marched until we came to the seashore, and Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah was our commander; then we found on the shore a whale that Allah had brought out for us. He said: it is only a carcass. Then Abu Ubaydah said: it is only provision Allah has provided you. He said: we stayed eighteen days, eating from its meat and oiling ourselves from its fat, while we were three hundred men. Abu Qatadah ordered a rib of that whale to be set up, and a rider passed beneath it."
"8438","From Ibn Uyaynah: he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me: he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent us, three hundred riders, our commander Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, to watch for the caravan of Quraysh. We went and stayed at the coast, and severe hunger befell us until we ate leaves. Then the sea cast out for us a creature called al-anbar, and we ate from it half a month, and oiled ourselves from its fat until our bodies recovered. Abu Ubaydah took a rib of it, looked at the tallest camel in the army and the tallest man among them, mounted him upon the camel, then he passed beneath the rib. A man of the people slaughtered three camels, then slaughtered three camels, then slaughtered three, then Abu Ubaydah forbade him. Amr said: I heard Abu Salih say: Qays ibn Sa'd said to his father: I was in the army and they hungered; he said: slaughter; he said: I have slaughtered; he said: then they hungered; he said: slaughter; he said: I have slaughtered; he said: then they hungered; he said: slaughter; he said: I have slaughtered; then they hungered; he said: slaughter; he said: I have been forbidden — and I think he was the one who slaughtered the camels that day."
"8439","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr: he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah narrate from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar; Jabir said: we mentioned it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: provision Allah brought out for you; if there is something of it with you, feed us. There was something of it with us, so some of the people sent to him and he ate from it. Jabir said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had provisioned us a bag of dates; he would take a handful for us from it, then a date at a time; we would suck it, then drink water over it until night; then what was in the bag ran out, and when it was finished we felt its loss."
"8440","Abd Allah ibn Umar and Ibn Jurayj reported, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Hurayrah asked him about fish cast up by the sea — is it a carcass? He said: yes, and forbade him eating it. When he entered the house he called for the mushaf and read: lawful to you is game of the sea and its food, as provision for you and for travelers. He said: then he sent to him and said: game of the sea and its food has been made lawful to you; what comes out of it, eat it, there is no harm in it, even if dead."
"8441","Ibn Jurayj said: Abu Bakr ibn Hafs informed me that Ibn Mas'ud said: the slaughter of fish is opening its jaws."
"8442","Ibn Jurayj said: Ata said: the rule of locusts is like the rule of fish regarding eating its dead."
"8443","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Ata: he said: if you strike a fish with your stick and kill it, or throw a stone at it and it dies, then eat it in every case; and locusts are like that."
"8444","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, from Ibn Abbas: he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought two roasted dabb-lizards, and Khalid ibn al-Walid was with him; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stretched his hand to eat it, and it was said: it is dabb; so he withheld his hand. Khalid said: is it forbidden? O Messenger of Allah! He said: no, but it is not in the land of my people, so I find myself disliking it. Then Khalid ate, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at him."
"8445","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', and Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the dabb? He said: I neither eat it nor forbid it."
"8446","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the dabb? He said: I do not eat it, nor do I forbid it. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd Allah, from Ibn Umar: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the same."
"8447","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought dabb meat, and he said: my father — or my forefathers — did not use to eat it. Khalid ibn al-Walid said: but my father used to eat it. Then Khalid ate from it, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at him."
"8448","Ibn Uyaynah reported: he said: Ali ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an informed me, from Umar ibn Harmalah, from Ibn Abbas: he said: the sister of Maymunah sent to her some dabb-lizards, or a dabb, and milk. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought some of those dabb-lizards, and he spat, and said to Khalid ibn al-Walid and ...: eat. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a vessel with milk, and he drank, while I was on his right; he said to me: the drink is yours; if you wish, O Ibn Abbas, to give Khalid preference with it, do so. I said: I will not give anyone preference with the leftover of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So I drank, then I gave to Khalid, and he drank. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: whomsoever Allah feeds food, let him say: O Allah, bless us in it, and feed us better than it; and whomsoever Allah gives milk to drink, let him say: O Allah, bless us in it, and increase us from it. He said: for I know nothing that suffices for food and drink except milk."
"8449","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib: that a man was a shepherd, and he complained to Umar ibn al-Khattab of hunger in his land; Umar said to him: are you not in a land abounding in dabb? He said: yes, O Commander of the Faithful. Umar said: I would not love to have red camels in exchange for dabb-lizards."
"8450","From Ma'mar, from Abu Harun al-Abdi, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri: he said: I heard him say: we, the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — for one of us to be gifted a roasted dabb was more beloved to him than a chicken."
"8451","From Ma'mar, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, or another — Ma'mar doubted — among the elders: he said: I heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri say: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a dabb, and he said: a tribe of the Children of Israel was lost, from among those Allah was angry with; if it be upon the earth, then it is this."
"8452","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me: he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a dabb, and he refused to eat it, saying: I do not know, perhaps it is of the early generations that were transformed. Abd al-Razzaq said: I narrated it to Ibrahim ibn Yazid, and he said: I heard Abu al-Zubayr and al-Walid ibn Abd Allah; they narrated it to us from Jabir."
"8453","From Ma'mar, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Abd Allah ibn Ubayd: he said: I asked Jabir ibn Abd Allah about the hyena, and he said: lawful. I said to him: from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: yes."
"8454","Ibn Jurayj reported: he said: Abd Allah ibn Ubayd informed me that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Ammar informed him: he said: I asked Jabir ibn Abd Allah about the hyena; he said: I said: shall I eat it? He said: yes. I said: is it game? He said: yes. I said: did you hear that from the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: yes."
"8455","Ibn Jurayj reported: he said: Nafi' informed me that a man informed Ibn Umar that Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas used to eat hyenas, and Ibn Umar did not disapprove it."
"8456","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: he said: Ali saw no harm in eating hyena, and made it game."
"8457","From Ma'mar, from Amr ibn Muslim: he said: I heard Ikrimah, the freedman of Ibn Abbas, and he was asked about it, and he said: I have seen it upon the table of Ibn Abbas."
"8458","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: he said: he was asked about the hyena, and he said: the Arabs have always eaten it."
"8459","From al-Thawri, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih: he said: a man of Syria came and asked Ibn al-Musayyib about eating hyena, and he forbade him. He said to him: your people eat it, or something like this. He said: my people do not know. Sufyan said: this saying is more beloved to me. I said to Sufyan: where is what came from Ibn Umar, Ali, and others? He said: has not the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidden eating every fanged beast of prey — so leaving it is more beloved to me. He said: and Abd al-Razzaq holds by it."
"8460","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Sa'di: he said: I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about eating hyena, and he said: eating it is not proper. An elder with him said: if you wish I will narrate to you what I heard from Abu al-Darda; he said: I heard him say: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade every plunder, and every snatching — meaning what is cut off the living — and every mujaththamah, and eating every fanged beast of prey. Sa'id said: you have spoken the truth."
"8461","Ma'mar reported, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about eating jerboa, and he saw no harm in it."
"8462","From Ma'mar: he said: I asked Ata al-Khurasani about the jerboa, kite, vultures, crows, and eagles? He said: nothing has reached me about them, and I do not forbid them, but I deem them filthy. Amr ibn Dinar said: I see no harm in eating them so long as you do not deem them filthy."
"8463","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: he was asked about eating jerboa, and he saw no harm in it."
"8464","From Ma'mar, from Asim, from al-Sha'bi: that Safwan ibn so-and-so, or so-and-so ibn Safwan, hunted two rabbits, and asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he ordered him to eat them."
"8465","And Ma'mar said: as for Jabir, he narrated to me, from al-Sha'bi: he said: Jabir ibn Abd Allah asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the rabbit, and he ordered him to eat it."
"8466","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, freedman of the family of Talhah, from Musa ibn Talhah, from a man of Banu Tamim called Ibn al-Hawtakiyyah, from Umar: that he said: which of us was present on the day of al-Qahah when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a rabbit? Abu Dharr said: I. He said: a Bedouin brought the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a rabbit, saying: I saw it bleeding. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: eat it. And he mentioned that he himself did not eat. The Bedouin said: I am fasting. He said: what is your fast? He mentioned a fast. He said: where are you regarding the white days? The thirteenth, fourteenth, and fifteenth."
"8467","From al-Thawri, from Harun, from Abd Allah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr, from his father: he said: a man came to him and asked him about the rabbit; he said: what forbids it? He said: they claim it menstruates. He said: when does it become pure? He said: I do not know. He said: he who knows when it menstruated knows when its purity is; Allah has left nothing except He has made it clear to you, lest it be forgotten. What Allah said is as Allah said, what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said is as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and what Allah and His Messenger did not say is by Allah's pardon and mercy — so leave it and do not investigate it; it is but a pregnant one among these pregnant ones."
"8468","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: he said: no harm in eating rabbit."
"8469","And I heard a man ask Ma'mar: did you hear Qatadah narrate, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that rabbits were served to Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas and Amr ibn al-As; Sa'd ate and Amr did not eat? And Ibn al-Musayyib said: we eat from what Sa'd ate, and pay no attention to what Amr did? Ma'mar said: yes, I heard Qatadah narrate it."
"8470","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Ubayd ibn Sa'd: he said: Bilal threw a stick at a rabbit, broke its legs, then slaughtered it and ate it."
